export const S = {"help_ws_s":"New orders appear on the start page under “New – please accept” – open and tap “Accept order”. If fleet management set a workshop date when approving, the “New workshop order” notification already shows it (“· Workshop date DD/MM/YYYY”). At the top of the start page the order flow is shown in short form (“How a workshop order works”); “Hide” collapses it, it can still be expanded. Sections such as “Planned for today” only highlight orders – the same order appears again below under its work step, marked “↑ also listed above”; the total number of open orders is given in the sentence above it.|“Start repair”, then record working time and parts (also from stock) in the case. External invoices are recorded by fleet management – pass the invoice or details on to them. Booked working time and parts cannot be changed afterwards – so the form shows the case and plate at the top (“Booking for”); check before saving. Booked wrongly anyway: write a comment in the case saying what is wrong (e.g. “2 h belong to case …”) so fleet management knows.|Warranty: if the invoice or supplier states a “warranty until” date, enter it for the part (for external services fleet management enters it with the external invoice). If the same vehicle is reported again, the new case shows the active warranty under “About the vehicle” – check a warranty claim. All affected open cases: Cases → “Check warranty”.|Fault comes back: “About the vehicle” lists this vehicle’s cases closed in the 90 days before, with their closing note (including “Ended without repair”) – e.g. read “no fault found last time” before starting the search from scratch.|Part moved from one of our own vehicles (parts donor): enter “Ausbau aus <plate>” as the part’s supplier and leave the price empty (unvalued, never €0); add a vehicle note “… removed” on the donor – it is shown when the vehicle returns to service. Parts usage also finds the installation by the donor’s plate.|Parts missing: “Waiting for parts” – fleet management and dispatch are informed; enter the supplier’s delivery date if known (later “Parts delivery date”) so dispatch can plan; then “Resume repair”. If a part arrives wrong or late: keep the status, set “Parts delivery date” to the new date and add the reason as a comment – this keeps the waiting time visible from the start. Following up: on the start page under “Waiting for parts” the longest-waiting order is at the top, with “Waiting for”, “Waiting” and “no delivery date given” – chase the supplier for that one first and enter the date they give. If only part of the order arrives: “Partial delivery received” – enter what is still outstanding (and the delivery date for the rest, if known); the order stays in “Waiting for parts”, the waiting time keeps running, and labour and fitted parts can already be booked. Only when everything is there: “Resume repair”.|Record work not done right away as open items; safety-relevant open items block completion.|Done: working time or an external service recorded and “Perform check” (post-repair check, 18 checkpoints; again after rework) – then “Send to final inspection” with a short summary as a note. Technical release is always given by a person from fleet management.|Enter a date when accepting or later use “Set/move date” – More → “Workshop plan” shows all dates per day, passed dates and jobs without a date; you can set the date there directly (“Suggest” only fills in the field and reuses an existing date of the same vehicle; “Vehicle also planned on” shows dates that could be combined). With several sites, “Vehicle site” filters by the vehicle’s site in the master data; the suggestion still takes all jobs of the workshop into account.|More → “Workshop plan”, section “Due – no date”: inspections and maintenance overdue or due soon by date or mileage (marked “km”, counted from the last recorded odometer reading) with neither an upcoming date nor an open job. Tap to open the vehicle file and plan a date there; the workshop job is created by fleet management.|Inspection (HU/SP/UVV/tachograph) done but result not yet recorded: the start page lists it under “Inspection result missing (recorded by you)” – tap it, choose the result from the inspection report, enter the report number as evidence, save. A recorded result is never overwritten.|Inspection with major/dangerous defects or “unroadworthy” without an order: the start page shows it for information under “Inspection with defects – no order yet”. Fleet management creates the order; it then appears like any other order.|Shared tablet: after work sign out via More → Sign out. If sign-out after inactivity is set, the app signs out by itself after that time without use; the previous person’s search, filters and sorting are discarded.|“No progress” (only if configured): own orders without change for the configured days – enter status, parts needed or a date as a comment or action.|“Winter tyres – trucks without Alpine symbol” on the start page: trucks without the Alpine symbol on a drive or front steering axle according to the records, as a work queue. Links go straight to Tyres – fleet (tabs “Winter not compliant” / “Winter not recorded”). Information only, no lock.|No key or registration certificate with the vehicle? In the order choose “Key / registration certificate” and enter “missing” (in the note e.g. where the key probably is) – fleet management and the reporter are notified. If everything is there, enter “at the workshop”.|Pick-up after repair: under “Ready for final inspection”, enter in “Pick-up after repair” where the vehicle, key and registration certificate are for pick-up (e.g. key box, compartment, parking space). Fleet management sees it at the release and can show it to the drivers.|Photo of the part number: take a photo of the type plate or part number under “Photos & documents” and select it under “Photo/PDF of the part” when adding the part – no need to type the number, and the photo helps with warranty and reordering.|Vehicle away for only a few hours (e.g. wheel alignment or brake test bench at an outside company)? Enter e.g. “mornings only, pick-up 1 pm, company …” under “Note on the date” – the workshop plan, calendar export and dispatch show it. Record the outside company’s costs as an external service with receipt; where the vehicle is right now goes under “Location / towing”.|Several orders on the same day appear under “Planned for today” and in the workshop plan in the same fixed order: locked and critical first, then medium, then low; with the same urgency the older report first. Comments or booked hours do not change the order. If fleet management has agreed a time or order, it is in the “Note on the date” (in brackets after the date).|Documents in a case (photos, invoices, inspection reports): at most 10 files per case, at most 5 MB per file (JPG, PNG, WEBP or PDF). From half full the case shows the free slots, when full a notice instead of the button – so upload multi-page documents as one PDF. Below each file you see the date/time and who uploaded it (role, name where permitted) – so damage photos from the report can be told apart from your own repair photos. “Add photos/PDF” offers camera, gallery and files to choose from.","help_fleet_s":"Open new reports and tap “Confirm receipt” – the reporter is informed.|Check the priority; for safety defects “Lock vehicle” (dispatch sees the lock immediately).|“Approve repair” and choose the workshop; reject only with a reason.|The “Management” tab shows all open cases and fleet agent findings that need action – short label, message and next step in your app language (entries such as inspection type, lock reason or note stay verbatim; the integration API and audit log keep the German text). A vehicle’s findings are also listed in its vehicle file under “Open agent findings”; the vehicle list shows their number per vehicle (and how many are critical) and filters with “With agent findings”. Under “To do today” on the overview, vehicles with critical agent findings form a separate group; “Show in vehicle list” opens the list with this filter, most critical first. In the “Management” tab, findings can be narrowed down by severity and search term; CSV export and printout then contain exactly this selection.|Inspection with dangerous defects or “unroadworthy” recorded: owner, fleet management and dispatch immediately receive an urgent notice (cannot be switched off; not for late entries whose inspection date is more than a month ago). The vehicle file shows “Inspection failed – no order yet” at the top with the button “Create defect repair order”; recording alone does not lock the vehicle (only the order with urgency “critical” does – preset for “dangerous defects” and “unroadworthy”) – fleet management decides on use and release.|Cases: with several sites, “Vehicle site” shows only cases of vehicles at that site (per master data) – combinable with search and status, also for the CSV export.|Duplicate report (same damage on the same vehicle): “Reject” and choose the existing case under “Duplicate of case” – the reason is prefilled, the reporter sees the number and the original case shows “Same report also as …”. Each duplicate shows its report time (e.g. driver A when parking, driver B when taking over). Reported on the other part of the combination (tractor instead of semi-trailer)? First “Correct vehicle”, then reject as a duplicate. When reporting, drivers also see the known defects of the permanently coupled semi-trailer or tractor.|No progress: if the owner set “Cases – follow up after days without change” in Settings, “Cases → No progress” lists triaged, accepted and in-repair cases with no change for that long (not even a comment). The fleet agent sends a quiet reminder. A short status comment is enough to clear it.|Follow-up: in a case, “Follow-up” sets your own date (and optionally a note). From that day the case appears under “To do today → Follow-up due” and the fleet agent sends a quiet reminder. Saving it empty removes it. Workshop and drivers do not see it.|Large lists: the case list first shows 200 (up to 500) cases, the CSV export up to 5000 – beyond that a notice appears; then narrow it down by status, search or “Vehicle site” and export separately.|Telematics (More → Telematics mapping): devices are assigned to a vehicle only by VIN or a confirmed mapping with evidence, never by plate. Marked red: the device reports a different plate than the assigned vehicle – check the device configuration at the provider or the vehicle master data; the app changes nothing. Below “Last data received” each provider shows when data last arrived and from how many devices – if one source stops, you see it there. If a device keeps reporting for a vehicle taken out of service, it also shows “⚠ out of service, still reporting: N” linking to data quality. “Latest data per vehicle” lists vehicles without data first, then the oldest data – old data may mean the vehicle was parked or the device failed; trailers only appear there if they have their own device (marked “Trailer”). “CSV export” there provides the same list with age in hours and any differing plate reported by the device, e.g. for a query to the provider. If a device reports fewer km than previously recorded, or more km than a later reading without a reason (e.g. data delivered late), the value is not taken over; “Latest data per vehicle” and the CSV show this per vehicle (⚠ km), and data quality shows “Telematics odometer inconsistent with recorded readings” (different device counter, wrong mapping or a typo in a manual reading). If a device keeps reporting after the vehicle was taken out of service, data quality shows “Telematics still reporting for vehicle taken out of service” – remove the device or deregister it with the provider (e.g. vehicle sold); remove a confirmed mapping under “Confirmed mappings” with “Remove mapping” – with VIN-based assignment this is only possible at the provider (deregister the device or correct the VIN there). The vehicle file of a vehicle taken out of service shows “Out of service since …” with reason and person from the log.|Changing a follow-up: the existing note is pre-filled in the form and kept when you move the date. A date that has since become due can be saved unchanged with a new note.|Comment with “Notify only”: for a new report, “Workshop” reaches the suggested workshop (it sees the case as a preview); without a selection it is not notified about new reports. After a rejection the “Workshop” option is no longer offered.|Asking the reporter (e.g. “please send a photo of the mirror”): comment with “Notify only: Reported by”. The driver can add photos/files to their open report. If that happens after fleet management has received it, the history shows “Photo/file added later” with the file name, and fleet management/owner get the notification “Photo/file added to the report” (not urgent, can be muted; several photos in a row give one notification). If the case already has a workshop order, the workshop is informed too. Photos added while reporting do not trigger a separate notification. With the tick “As a question to the reporter (answer expected)” the comment becomes a question: the reporter gets “Question about your report” (cannot be muted) and sees it at the top of their start page; the case shows “Question open” until the reporter comments or adds a file, then “Reporter has answered” – also under “To do today” – until fleet management or the owner acts on the case again. There is no deadline; set a follow-up to chase it.|Vehicle documents (vehicle file → Documents): upload registration, insurance, leasing etc. with “valid until”. Chips show “expired”, “expires soon” (advance warning as for inspection dates) and “superseded” (newer document of the same type). If the same file is uploaded again with different details, it is not stored twice – “Correct document details” opens with the new details.|Management → “Open decisions”: if the same finding occurs again, only the newest entry counts; older open entries of that finding are hidden and settled together with “Resolved” on the newest. “Seen” marks the entry; afterwards it shows “seen by …” with the time. “Run agent check now” starts a check immediately; if the agent has not run as scheduled for a while, a warning appears at the top.|Telematics mapping → “Cases to check (unmapped)”: devices without a VIN match and without a confirmed mapping. Choose the “Vehicle”, enter “Evidence / source” (e.g. the provider’s device list) and “Confirm mapping” – “Plate matches” is only a suggestion and is not preselected. At most 500 cases are shown (the newest). “Remove mapping” under “Confirmed mappings” also removes the device data from the vehicle; recorded odometer readings stay as evidence. Mapped devices write one odometer reading per vehicle and day (source “Telematics”) to the vehicle file – it counts for “Mileage per month”, €/km and mileage-based due dates. The vehicle file shows time, km, ignition and position under “Latest telematics status” (also for dispatch).|Changing a vehicle’s site (vehicle file → “Edit master data”): open workshop orders stay with the previous workshop, planned inspections remain unchanged and the coupled vehicle is not changed with it – the app shows this as a note after saving. Fleet management can hand over an order in status “Approved for workshop” to the new workshop with “Change workshop”. The cost report and KPIs assign earlier months to today’s site from the vehicle master data as well (there is no site history).|Total loss, sale or uneconomic repair: in the case use “End without repair” with a reason – the workshop order ends without technical release (status “rejected”), workshop, reporter and dispatch are notified, booked costs remain. Clarify a safety lock and safety-relevant remaining tasks on their merits first. Afterwards the vehicle can be taken out of service.|Vehicle towed or no longer at the reported place: in the case use “Location / towing” to enter where it is now, which towing service took it and when – only as stated by the driver, the towing company or a document. Dispatch and the workshop see the location in the case and under “Do not plan”; the case list marks open cases as “towed”. Book the towing costs with “External invoice”.|Damage reported later (e.g. on Monday for Friday): when reporting, “When did it happen?” takes the day and – if known – the time; later the management enters or corrects it in the case with “Time of damage”. The case then shows how many days later it was reported and which drivers were on the vehicle at that time according to the vehicle assignment or a departure check – only as a hint for follow-up questions, not an assignment. Without an entry the field stays empty (never derived from the report time); the case and insurance claim CSVs contain the column “Time of damage as reported”.|Report on the wrong vehicle (plates mixed up): “Correct vehicle” in the case – as long as the report has not been released to a workshop. Case, photos and any safety lock move to the correct vehicle; drivers and dispatch of both vehicles are informed. With duplicates, follow-up orders or an insurance claim: reject and report again. Defect from a departure check: the whole check with all defects moves along, the odometer reading only if it fits the correct vehicle.|Vehicles swapped on the road: if a driver reports on a vehicle they were not assigned to, the case shows “Reporter’s assignment: not assigned to this vehicle” with their own vehicle and the people assigned to this vehicle. Clarify with the driver and change the assignment in the vehicle file if needed – the app changes nothing by itself.|Key / registration certificate: if the workshop reports that the vehicle key or the registration certificate is missing, fleet management and the reporter get a notification; the case shows it under “Key / registration certificate”, the case list and the workshop plan mark open cases with “Key missing” or “Registration missing”. The vehicle file and dispatch (“Do not plan”) show it as well; the “Technically release” form warns while something is still recorded as missing. Once resolved, enter “at the workshop”.|Pick-up after repair: the “Technically release” form shows the workshop’s pick-up note (where vehicle, key and registration certificate are) and lets you correct it. “Also show to this vehicle’s drivers …” creates a “vehicle note” for the assigned drivers (optionally with “valid until”, otherwise until archived). The note appears in the case and in the vehicle file until the vehicle goes to a workshop again – never in the push text (lock screen).|Limits at a glance: 10 photos per report, 10 files per case, 20 files per fine, 30 documents and 20 active notes per vehicle, 50 active emergency numbers per environment; at most 5 MB per file. From half full the app shows the free slots, when full a notice instead of the form – make room by archiving resolved notes or numbers that are no longer valid.","help_release_s":"Open cases in status “Final inspection”: the workshop has already carried out the post-repair check (18 checkpoints) in status “In repair” (“Perform check”) – the result is shown in the case.|Defects from the inspection automatically become follow-up orders.|“Technically release” lifts this case’s lock – if another open case locks the vehicle, it stays locked; if there are problems, “Request rework” – after the rework the workshop carries out a new check.|Open non-safety items stay visible and are reported by the agent.","help_dates_s":"More → Inspection dates: overdue and upcoming HU/SP/UVV/tachograph, gap list, CSV and calendar export. With several sites, the “Site” selection shows only that site’s vehicles (also for CSV, calendar and print).|Joint appointment (owner, fleet management, workshop): below the list open “Joint appointment for several inspections”, choose a date, tick entries of the current view (e.g. view “Due, not scheduled”, then all UVV) and press “Set date for selection”. Entries with a different arrangement from today stay unchanged and are listed – “Replace existing appointments” replaces them. Vehicles out of service, completed recalls and HU/SP without their own entry (plan individually in the vehicle file) are not planned. Working through: in the workshop plan an inspection opens the completion form in the vehicle file directly; after saving you return to the plan for the next vehicle.|Inspection for several vehicles (owner, fleet management): at the top of Inspection dates, “Create inspection for several vehicles” – type, label (e.g. “Winter check 2026 – management instruction”), optional due date and interval, choose vehicles. Each vehicle gets its own entry; record the completion and odometer per vehicle with “Done” afterwards. Not created and listed individually: out of service, dormant, type already present. Sending again creates no duplicates. The app calculates HU/SP itself; recalls via “Import recalls”.|Enter dates only with evidence; “Done” keeps every completion in the history.|Odometer readings with reading date; deviations only with a reason (e.g. odometer replaced).|If a reading is lower than the previous one (e.g. odometer replaced, saved with a reason), the app does not mix counter readings: mileage-based inspections whose data were last changed before that date show “Mileage due unclear” (status “Deadline unclear”, notice under “Management”) until the service is recorded with the new counter’s reading or the mileage due is re-entered with evidence. A contract mileage allowance shows no value after a drop since the contract start (clarify the odometer replacement record with the contract partner); the forecast never spans such a drop. “Done” with a reading below the previously recorded one is rejected (typo) – if the odometer was replaced, first record the new reading with a reason; drops without a reason (e.g. older entries) are listed under More → Data quality. Planning an appointment does not clarify the mileage due. If the inspection is overdue or due soon by date, it still appears under “Due, not scheduled” and in the workshop plan – with the notice instead of remaining km.|More → Data quality (owner/fleet management) checks vehicle master data, assignments, invitations, driver files/downloads, contracts, inspection results and defects, odometer readings and telematics for contradictions, gaps and notes. Filter at the top by severity and check, with several sites by “Vehicle site”; “CSV export” and “Print / PDF” follow the selection. Nothing is changed automatically.|Equipment (fire extinguisher, first-aid kit, load securing) and bodies (tail lift, loader crane) can be added as an inspection type in the vehicle file – enter interval or expiry date per label/package or inspection log; tail lift and loader crane each have their own date per vehicle.|Reefer trailers: “Reefer unit (service)”, “ATP certificate” and “Temperature recorder” are separate inspection types – enter the interval or expiry date per service record, ATP certificate/plate or test report; the app does not set any deadlines.|Manufacturer recall: inspection type “Recall (manufacturer)”, evidence = letter or reference number, deadline only if stated in the letter; once carried out it shows “done” (no interval); the vehicle list shows open recalls and filters with “Recall open”. Manufacturer list (VIN + reference): More → Inspection dates → “Import recalls”.|Workshop order: in the vehicle file at the inspection, “Create workshop order” – the order goes straight to the workshop and a planned date is taken over. After completion record the inspection with evidence; if missing, “To do today” reminds you.|“Completed inspections (evidence list)” at the bottom of Inspections: choose period and type, Show or CSV – e.g. for authorities or insurers.|Inspection with major/dangerous defects: enter the result when recording it. Without a follow-up case it appears in “To do today”; in the vehicle file under “Previous completions” use “Create defect repair order” – with urgency “critical” (preset for “dangerous defects” and “unroadworthy”) the vehicle is locked. Record the re-inspection afterwards with evidence – after the order is released, “To do today” reminds you (“Defects repaired – record the re-inspection”) until a newer inspection of the same type is recorded. For HU/SP with major defects or worse there is no sticker: leave “Due date per sticker/report” empty (the deadline for presenting the vehicle again is stated in the inspection report) and after the re-inspection record the new inspection with result and the due date per sticker – the form points this out.|Inspection with minor defects: for HU/SP the sticker is issued, enter the due date per the new sticker. Repair the defects listed in the report without delay, if needed via “Report damage” (linked under “Previous completions”). The app sets no deadline for this and sends no reminder.|Tachograph (inspection type “Tachograph”, § 57b StVZO, trucks only): the evidence is the installation plaque or inspection certificate; enter the next inspection per the plaque (the app never sets a date itself). If the entry is missing or has no date, it appears in the gap list and the driver sees “deadline unclear – fleet management will clarify” under “My vehicle”. After a gearbox replacement or a changed tyre size the app points out that an inspection is due.|If a newer completion of the same inspection type is recorded later (re-inspection, also after a repair outside the app), the defect of the older inspection counts as resolved; if the newer one shows defects again, the app reminds you of that one. “Previous completions” shows the re-inspection status for every inspection with defects.|Forgot the result? In “Previous completions” use “Add inspection result” at the newest completion (with evidence, e.g. report no.). A recorded result is never overwritten; More → Data quality and “To do today” list mandatory inspections without a result; in the evidence list, “Add result ›” jumps straight to the form.|Inspections by mileage show “Forecast: approx. <date>” once at least two odometer readings 14 days apart exist (within 90 days, latest no older than 90 days) – extrapolated from past mileage. Planning aid only: status, reminders and due point still follow the actual odometer reading.|Filter “Forecast ≤ 30 days”: all mileage-based due points expected within the next 30 days, sorted by date – to schedule the workshop in time.|Mileage-based service (e.g. oil change): “due at km” as shown by the service indicator or a document, or mileage at the last service + interval km – in the “Add due date” form and in the inspection import (columns “Faellig bei km”, “km bei letzter Pruefung”, “Intervall km”). HU/SP depend on time only. If the base value is missing, Data quality shows “Mileage service without base km”.|Tab “Due by mileage” (shown only with matches): all mileage rules exceeded or within the advance warning, fewest remaining km first. Workshop plan → “Due – no date” collects what has neither a date nor an open job; the home page links straight there.|HU inspector on the yard (Backlog 620): in the vehicle record under HU “Record inspection” the date is prefilled with the planned date. If the SP was done the same day with the same report, tick “SP carried out the same day” and choose the SP result – one save stores HU and SP. Attach the inspection report (PDF/photo) right there; it is filed as document “Inspection report”. After major or dangerous defects the vehicle list shows “Re-inspection open” and no new deadline is shown until the re-inspection is recorded.","help_dispo_s":"Do not plan locked vehicles (red plate) – only the technical release or a justified “Lift lock” by fleet management lifts the lock – with several locking cases only once all of them are released or lifted.|“Waiting for parts” notices mean a vehicle will be out longer. The case then shows “Waiting for” (which parts) and – if the delivery date was postponed, e.g. because wrong parts were delivered – how often, the original date and the latest note on it.|The vehicle list shows for each vehicle: locked, in workshop, report open or “No open reports”. The latter only checks reports and locks of this vehicle – not the coupled vehicle, inspection deadlines or appointments. For a replacement vehicle also check the notes in the row and the links “Combination locked” and “Inspection/maintenance overdue”.|Filter “Combination locked”: trucks or trailers whose coupled vehicle (per master list) is locked – do not plan the combination.|The home page shows “Planned dates (7 days)”: workshop and inspection dates entered by the workshop or fleet management – More → “Workshop plan” shows all dates. “Vehicle also planned on …” means the vehicle is out on several days – consider combining with the workshop. “🔗 SZ-… planned on …” lists dates of the permanently coupled vehicle – the combination is then also out on several days.|“Inspection failed – no order yet”: a recorded inspection with dangerous defects or unroadworthy – do not schedule the vehicle until fleet management has decided. New inspections of this kind trigger an urgent notification immediately (not for late entries with an inspection date more than a month ago).|The vehicle list also shows the status of the permanently coupled trailer on the truck (e.g. “Trailer SZ-… : locked”) and vice versa – do not plan the combination then. The vehicle’s own availability stays unchanged.|With “My default site” (More → My account) the start page shows only vehicles and cases of that site; the note at the top switches to “all sites”.|Vehicle file → “Vehicle notes”: short pinned notes (e.g. “upper deck sticks – known”). Owner, fleet management, workshop and dispatch can add them and archive finished ones; when ticked, assigned drivers see them on “My vehicle”. With “Valid until” a note disappears by itself after that day (it stays in the archive). The start page shows “Vehicles with notes”, and the vehicle list has a matching filter.|“Winter tyres – trucks not winter-ready” on the start page: trucks without the Alpine symbol on a drive or front steering axle according to the records. Information only, no lock – do not plan them in slippery conditions. Unrecorded tyres are never assumed to be winter tyres. If it says “Winter tyres – recording incomplete”, no truck is recorded as not winter-ready, but tyre details are missing for some (number below) – whether those trucks are winter-ready is open; ask fleet management.|Towed vehicles: “Do not plan” and the case show “towed” and the entered location (towing company yard, external workshop). Fleet management and the workshop enter it – please pass on new details from the driver to them.|“New reports, receipt not yet confirmed (n)” on the home page: reports fleet management has not yet reviewed – only fleet management confirms receipt. “Return: unclear”: the workshop date is the start of the repair, not its completion – if the repair is already under way or the date (or the expected parts delivery date) is today or has passed, the return is unclear; ask the workshop or fleet management if needed.|A driver calls: record the report via “Report” – you are then listed as the reporter and receive the updates (e.g. forwarding to the workshop); inform the driver of the decision yourself. “No, not drivable” or priority “Critical” locks the vehicle immediately. The hints shown while reporting (e.g. “do not continue driving”) are meant for the driver on the phone.|The “Note on the date” (e.g. “mornings only”) is entered by the workshop or fleet management. Whether the vehicle can still be used before or after the date, clarify with them – the app does not calculate times.|“Vehicle location” shows “as of …” (when last entered). If it is older, e.g. after a wheel alignment at an outside company, check with the workshop or fleet management – the app never changes the location by itself.|“Back in service (released today/yesterday)” on the start page: vehicles whose case was technically released today or yesterday and that no other open case blocks – with the release time, workshop and “Pick-up after repair” (where keys and registration papers are). A vehicle still listed under “Do not plan” does not appear here.|“Can be planned until the workshop date”: vehicles with an issued workshop order whose date is after today – the workshop has not started, the vehicle is not locked, not reported as “not driveable” and no location or towing is entered. According to the system it can be used until the day before the date (plan the trip so it reaches the workshop on the day; check the “Note on the date”). In the fleet bar they still count under “In workshop”. Date today or passed, work started or vehicle locked → “Do not plan”.|If the workshop or fleet management moves or removes a date that lies between today and 7 days ahead, or moves it into that period, you receive the notification “Workshop date changed” with the new date (push can be switched off under Alerts → “Push for these notices”).|“Reported as driveable with restrictions, no workshop order yet (n)” leads to reports that are neither locked nor ordered – for information only; agree with fleet management whether to plan the vehicle.|Which notifications (and push) dispatch receives: new reports that are critical, reported as “not driveable” or locked; upgrade to “critical”; lock, lock lifted, technical release and “remains locked”; “Waiting for parts”; “Workshop date changed”; order ended without repair; failed inspection (dangerous defects, unroadworthy). No notification – start page only: normal reports and reports “driveable with restrictions”, as well as overdue inspections and maintenance. So open the start page at the start of the shift and before planning.","help_myveh_s":"Under “My vehicle” the home page shows the vehicle fleet management assigned to you – with trailer and last odometer reading.|If it says “do not drive” (red), the vehicle is locked: do not set off, call dispatch.|“Already reported (open)” lists known defects – do not report them again; if it has got worse, report it anew.|“Workshop date” is the date entered by the workshop – coordinate via dispatch. If a date within the next 7 days is moved or cancelled, you receive the notification “Workshop date changed”.|Below are the next inspection dates (e.g. HU, SP) with due date; “planned” means a workshop date has been entered.|If the permanently coupled trailer (or towing vehicle) is locked, the top shows “Trailer …: locked – do not drive” – do not set off with this combination either; call dispatch.|A yellow box with ⓘ is a note from fleet management/workshop about this vehicle (e.g. a known quirk, spare key) – please take note.|Open items still pending (vehicle is released): if the workshop released the truck and left items open that are not safety-relevant, they are listed here with the case number – also for the coupled trailer or tractor unit. Do not report them again, only if something gets worse.|Where is the truck? If fleet management or the workshop entered “Vehicle location” or a towing service in the case, it is shown with the known defects with 📍 and when it was recorded – e.g. for picking it up at an external company. If a push message “Vehicle technically released” ends with “Pick-up note: My vehicle”, the notes on the vehicle say where the key and registration certificate are. If a known defect has a workshop order, the workshop is shown there and – if reported so – “reported as driveable with restrictions”; the planned workshop date shows the workshop next to the date (e.g. if you take the truck there yourself).","help_report_s":"Tap “Report” at the bottom.|Choose the vehicle – your assigned vehicles are at the top, trailers are marked.|Briefly describe the problem (where on the vehicle, what happened).|Choose urgency and whether the vehicle can be driven.|Optionally add photos (up to 10) and tap “Send report”.|You get a case number; follow the status under “Cases”.|If several notes match the description, the most important one (e.g. smoke/fire, accident, flat tyre) is open; the others are listed below and can be expanded with “Show steps”.|Owner/fleet management: same damage on several vehicles (e.g. hail in the yard) – choose “Same damage on several vehicles” at the top and tick up to 40 vehicles; each vehicle gets its own case, add photos afterwards in the respective case.|Question from fleet management: if fleet management still needs information or a photo, “Question from fleet management” appears at the top of your start page. Open the case, read the question in the history and answer below as a comment or add a photo – then fleet management sees that the answer has arrived.","help_prio_s":"Low: cosmetic or minor, trip not affected.|Medium: should be repaired soon, driving possible with care.|Critical: safety affected (e.g. brakes, steering, tyres, load securing). The vehicle is locked immediately.|Driveability “No, not drivable” also locks the vehicle immediately – even with low or medium urgency.|For critical damage, also call dispatch immediately.","help_check_s":"Open “Pre-trip check” (bottom bar: “Check”) and choose the vehicle.|Rate every item: OK, defect or “n/a”.|Describe each defect briefly; mark safety-relevant defects.|Enter the odometer and save – defects automatically become work orders. If an order from a check is already open for the same item (including one another order was rejected as a duplicate of), no new order is created; instead it gets the note “Found again” – for a safety-relevant defect only if the open order is critical and already locks the vehicle (otherwise a new, locking order).|“My vehicle” shows ❄ winter tyres if, according to the records, there is no Alpine symbol on a drive or front steering axle: do not set off in slippery conditions and inform dispatch. If this is wrong (e.g. new tyres with the Alpine symbol), inform dispatch or fleet management – the workshop or fleet management records the marking in the vehicle file.","help_push_s":"Under “Alerts”, enable push for this device and allow it.|iPhone/iPad: first in Safari “Share → Add to Home Screen”, then open the app from the home screen and enable push.|Enable it on each device separately.|“Send test” checks delivery to this device and arrives immediately, even during your quiet hours (if push is not enabled on this device, the test is held until they end). If the app reports “device unregistered by the push service”, enable push on this device again.|Under “Alerts → Push for these notices” you can deselect individual non-urgent types – they still appear in the list, just without a push. Urgent and safety notifications cannot be switched off.|If a vehicle assigned to you is locked, you receive an urgent “Vehicle locked” notification (for a new critical report by someone else: “CRITICAL: new report (unverified)”) – do not drive until “Lock lifted” or “Vehicle technically released” arrives.|Quiet hours (e.g. at night or during your rest period): enter from–to under “Alerts → Push for these notices”. Only urgent pushes arrive then; all other notifications appear in the app straight away and the push follows as one summary when quiet hours end.|The list under “Alerts” shows the newest 100 per tab; the numbers on the tabs and on the bell count all of them. Older unread ones are under “Unread”, “Mark all read” covers all of them.|If a push arrives while the alerts are open and you are entering something (e.g. quiet hours), the page stays as it is; “Refresh list” appears at the top.|Tapping a notification or push opens its subject directly: the case, the vehicle file for dangerous inspection defects or tyres below minimum tread, the fine for a letter from an authority, and “My sign-ins (devices)” for a new sign-in. Delayed pushes show the time of the event.|Language (More → My account): notifications and pushes arrive in the selected language. After a change, the list also shows older notifications in the new language, and delayed pushes (after quiet hours or retries) arrive in it too. Pushes already received on the device stay as they are; free text (e.g. comments) is not translated.","help_offline_s":"Reports and pre-trip checks are stored on the device when there is no connection.|They are sent automatically once the network is back – keep the app open or reopen it later. Sending only happens while the same person is signed in on the device – after signing out (including after inactivity) only once they sign in here again.|Photos (up to 10) are also stored on the device and sent automatically with the report or afterwards; for the pre-trip check only with a defect (attached to the follow-up order); if the defect was already reported by someone else (“Found again”), the photos are not uploaded. Only if the app reports that photos could not be stored, attach them in the case after sending. A stored pre-trip check is accepted no later than 7 days after it was saved – after that, carry it out again.|For critical damage, do not wait – call.|Slow network: the button is dimmed while sending – tapping again does not send twice, just wait a moment.|Entries in other fields on the same page are kept when you save; only the form just sent is cleared.|New phone: before switching, open the old phone with a connection until nothing is left in the outbox – saved reports/checks cannot be moved to another device. Then sign in on the new phone, turn on push and under My account → My sign-ins choose “Sign out all other devices”: the old phone then no longer receives notifications. Old phone lost: sign out the same way (or ask fleet management) and re-enter reports that were not yet sent.","help_drivers_s":"More → Drivers: add drivers (also without an app account) and record documents – expiry dates only as stated in the document.|A new licence, driver card or Code 95 record replaces the previous one. Instructions and other records apply per title: a newer record with the same title (e.g. “Load securing”) replaces the older one, other titles are monitored separately.|Driver card reported damaged, lost or stolen: the previous card stays in the file until the replacement is recorded as a new “Driver card (tachograph)” record – it replaces the old one. The download deadline continues per driver, not per card.|Record with a typo (e.g. expiry 2036 instead of 2026): in the driver file, “Cancel” it with a reason – it stays crossed out in the history but no longer counts; the newest remaining record applies again. Then enter the correct one.|Record licence checks with date and result; a reminder is only given if an interval is set in the settings.|More → Tachograph downloads: after each download record the date and evidence (e.g. file name) – individually, in the vehicle file or as a CSV import from the download software.|Deadlines per FPersV § 2 (5): driver card at least every 28 days, vehicle unit at least every 90 days. Without a recorded download the app calculates no deadline.|Overdue and upcoming downloads appear on the home page under “To do today” and as a quiet summary reminder.|Download recorded by mistake (e.g. a typo in the date): in the history of the driver or vehicle file, “Cancel” it with a reason – it stays visible crossed out but no longer counts for the deadline; then record the correct day.|For a company audit: Downloads → “History for a period” shows all recorded downloads from–to per driver card and tachograph (including drivers who have left and vehicles handed back) with every deadline breach in days; as printout/PDF or CSV. Records and licence checks of all drivers for a period: More → Drivers → “Records and licence checks for a period” (status today per record, two CSV files).|Annual training with many participants (owner, fleet management): More → Drivers → “Record training for several drivers” – enter topic, date, optionally “valid until” (only as stated in the evidence) and evidence (e.g. attendance list) once, tick the participants (“All”/“None”). Each driver gets a “Training” record in the driver file; an identical entry already recorded (topic, date, evidence) is not duplicated and is reported as “already recorded”.|Licence checks for many drivers on one day: More → Drivers → “Licence check for several drivers (OK)” – date, optional note, “Select due” (due, due soon or no check yet) or tick individually. Only for checks without findings; record defects or “not presented” individually in the driver file with a note. Sending again creates no duplicates.","help_stock_s":"More → Spare parts stock: add items with quantity, minimum stock, storage location and – if known – net price.|Open an item → “Edit item”: correct name, part number, minimum stock, location and price, or deactivate the item (it disappears from the stock list, reorder proposal and stocktake, keeps its history and can be reactivated). Quantities never change here and the unit is fixed; every change is logged.|In a job choose “Add part” → “From stock”: the quantity is deducted from stock immediately and the price is taken from the stock item.|Book receipts (deliveries), withdrawals and corrections on the item; a correction always needs a reason. Negative stock is not possible.|Stocktake: enter counted quantities, “Check differences”, then “Book differences” – empty fields count as not counted.|“Last counted”: every item counted in a booked or completed stocktake gets the date and counted quantity – shown on the item, in the stocktake list, on the count sheet and in the CSV stock list. This shows which items were never or not recently counted. Only booked counts count, not the check preview. For a rolling stocktake the count sheet can list only items not counted for 90/180/365 days or never.|“Reorder list” shows all items below minimum stock with the shortfall and usage over the last 90 days (CSV or print). The app orders nothing – ordering from the supplier works as before.|“Stock journal”: all receipts, withdrawals and corrections of a period (e.g. a month) with case, document and value – view, CSV or print. Value only with a stored price; the person who posted is not listed.|The reorder list shows the last supplier for each item (from the most recent receipt with a supplier) and the date of the last receipt; per stock it is sorted by supplier so you can order per supplier. Without a posted supplier the field stays empty.|“Show” above the item list: “below minimum” or “no withdrawal for 180/365 days” (items in stock only, never withdrawn ones included) – with the tied-up value of priced items; helps tidy up the stock. Each row shows the last withdrawal.|Transfer: in the item use “Transfer to another stock” – choose the target stock and quantity. The app books out here and in there (same item by part number/name, otherwise newly created); transfers do not count as consumption and are listed separately in the stock journal.|“Note an order” in the item (quantity, optionally the supplier’s delivery date): the reorder list then shows “ordered … on …” and “still to order” – avoiding double orders. The note ends with the next receipt that covers the noted quantity or via “Remove note”; after a partial delivery it stays with the remaining quantity (delivery date unchanged) – if the rest will not come, “Remove note”. If the delivery date has passed, the fleet agent reports “delivery date passed”.|Stocktake: Spare parts stock → “Stocktake” → “Print count sheet” (sorted by location, by default without book stock = blind count). Enter counted quantities (empty = not counted), “Check differences”, then “Book differences” – the app asks first. If a quantity or the stock changes afterwards (e.g. withdrawal in a job), nothing is booked: check again. If everything matches, “Complete stocktake” records the count without booking; “Last stocktake” shows date and result per stock. “Stock at key date” (e.g. 31 Dec) recalculates the stock from all bookings up to the end of that day – with the last purchase price up to the key date and today’s item price; accounting decides which valuation applies. Booked differences are shown under “Stocktake differences” (book stock → counted, value at today’s item price; without a price unvalued) – also as CSV and printout.|History in the item: the latest 200 postings are shown there. “Show in stock journal” opens the stock journal for this item only (last 12 months, period can be changed, CSV/print); “All items” removes the restriction.|Stock value: below the filter, the sum of stock × stored net price is shown for the items displayed. Items in stock without a price count as “unvalued” (not €0). For the annual accounts use “Stock at key date” – the valuation method is set by accounting.|Import items (CSV): choose a stock list with Bezeichnung (name, required), Teilenummer, Einheit, Bestand, Mindestbestand, Lagerort and Preis. The preview shows new, existing and skipped rows; only “Apply” creates new items. Existing items (same name and part number, case-insensitive) are never changed – book their stock as a receipt or stocktake.|“Parts usage (recall)” (stock, or “Where fitted?” in the item): for a supplier recall or warranty case enter the part number, name, supplier or document, optionally the fitting period – the app lists all fittings according to the cases with vehicle, date, quantity, supplier, document and warranty (view, CSV, print; workshop only jobs of its own site). Whether a part has since been removed is unknown to the app. In a case, “Fitted elsewhere?” next to the part number leads straight there. Matching items still in stock are listed there under “Still in stock” (check before use).|“Goods receipt” (stock): post a delivery with several lines in one step – supplier and delivery note once, item, quantity and optional purchase price per line; all lines are posted together (or none), order notes end as with a single receipt (partial delivery: the remaining quantity stays noted). Afterwards the page shows this workshop’s orders waiting for parts with their note – “Note matches” if it names the supplier, a delivered item or its part number; then choose “Resume repair” or “Partial delivery received” in the order. Owner/fleet management can create an open recall for the vehicles found via “Record a recall for the affected vehicles” (reference and deadline only as per the letter, preview, then apply) – it stays in inspections and the workshop plan until it is recorded as done.","help_privacy_s":"More → Users → “Data access export” in the person’s row downloads all data stored for this account as a file; for drivers without an app account use the driver file. Only the owner can create a data access export.|Passwords, login data and push keys are not included. Nothing is changed or deleted.|The file contains personal data: hand it only to the person concerned and check it for third-party data first (e.g. names in comments).|Every export is logged.","help_defects_s":"More → “Check defect statistics”: choose the period (30–365 days) and type – departure checks, post-repair checks or both. With several sites, a site can also be selected.|“By check item”: how often an item was recorded as a defect and on how many vehicles. Many vehicles with the same item point to a general topic (e.g. part quality, maintenance plan).|“Again after repair”: new defects on an item that was repaired and released on the same vehicle within the 90 days before – a reason to review the repair or the part.|“By vehicle”: checks, checks with defects and number of defects – look at vehicles with many defects in more detail via the vehicle file.|Only stored checks of this environment are counted; dormant vehicles are excluded. The statistics do not assess people. Use “Print / PDF” for meetings.|“CSV per check item” and “CSV per vehicle” download the analysis shown (period, check type, site) for Excel – per vehicle with all checked vehicles, including those without defects.","help_contracts_s":"Vehicle file → “Add contract”: type, partner and evidence are required; enter dates, notice period, mileage allowance and rate only as stated in the contract.|With a mileage allowance, enter the km at handover per the handover protocol – the app shows km driven and remaining plus a linear forecast to the contract end.|More → Contracts lists all contracts needing action (end, notice day, allowance); the fleet agent reminds you in advance (advance warning in Settings).|After return or termination use “End contract” with date and reason – the contract stays in the file as evidence.|Contract document: first upload it under “Documents” (e.g. type Leasing/rental), then select it in the contract under “Contract document” – the file links to it directly.|More → “Costs per vehicle” shows contract rates separately from maintenance costs (per started month up to today, only with start date and rate).|With several sites, “Vehicle site” filters the list, rate total, CSV and calendar; the preset is “My default site” from My account. The same applies to insurance claims, tachograph downloads and drivers.|If the odometer goes backwards (e.g. tachograph replaced), the allowance is “not calculable” and the fleet agent reports a notice – check the km at handover against the document and confirm “km at handover checked …” in the contract; the app then calculates again.|Monthly report to the lessor: choose a key date at the top (e.g. the last day of the month). Only odometer readings up to the end of that day then count, each line shows the reading date and, if exceeded, the excess km. Without a reading up to the key date it says “no km reading” – the app projects nothing. The CSV contains the same values (columns for odometer, reading date, excess/shortfall km, km status and key date).|Returning leased/rented vehicles: if a leasing or rental contract ends soon (advance warning per setting) or has expired, the overview shows “Before return” with the open cases and the open remaining jobs of already released cases on the vehicle – so damage can be repaired before the return. The fleet agent gives the same numbers in the finding “Contract ending soon”, the CSV in two separate columns. When ending a leasing/rental contract in the vehicle file, the form shows the same information and the latest odometer reading – if it is not from today, record the odometer reading at return first. If someone reports damage to a leased/rented vehicle, the vehicle summary in the case (management only) names the running contract – many contracts require reporting damage to the contract partner or using specific workshops. If a maintenance or full-service contract is running, the summary names it too – check the scope of services in the contract before releasing to your own workshop. The “Create workshop order” form for inspection dates in the vehicle file shows the same.|Annual planning (e.g. in December for Q1): More → Contracts → “by end of next quarter” or “by end of quarter”, or a date under “End/notice by” – the list then shows only running contracts whose end or notice deadline as recorded falls by then; print and CSV likewise. Contracts without a recorded end do not appear there.|Several contracts at once (Backlog 624): More → Contracts → “End several contracts or enter a contract end”. Tick the running contracts of the current view (e.g. choose type “Insurance”), then “Enter contract end” (also in the future, e.g. 31/12 for an insurer change) or after the end “End” with a reason. Contracts not processed are listed with the reason. If a follow-up contract is recorded, the agent finding names it.","help_claims_s":"In the case (e.g. accident) use “Record insurance claim”: select the insurance contract or enter the insurer; reporting date and evidence are required.|Enter claim number, deductible and status only as stated in the insurer’s letter.|Enter the reimbursement with amount and date per the settlement – the balance (documented costs minus reimbursement) appears under More → Insurance claims – only once all cost items are documented; otherwise it shows “Documented costs – incomplete”.|The overview shows open claims with “open for … days” and can be exported as CSV.|Reporting and reimbursement appear in the vehicle file timeline; the cost report lists reimbursements separately (not deducted from costs).|Data quality points out cases whose description mentions “accident” but have no insurance claim yet.|Follow-up reminder with the insurer: the owner enters the number of days under Settings (empty = off).|Claims of dormant vehicles stay visible (marked “Dormant”) but trigger no follow-up reminder; their reimbursements are shown on a separate line, not in the total.|Appraiser: enter appraiser/office, inspection date (planned allowed), date the appraisal was received and the damage amount per appraisal – all optional and only per appointment/letter. If an inspection is entered but no appraisal has arrived yet, the list shows “Appraisal pending” (own filter, CSV column).|Accident with another vehicle: enter the other party’s plate and insurer together with their claim number under “Other party” – only as stated in the accident report or a letter. Names and addresses go in “Note” for now.|Theft or break-in: the insurer usually requires the police report – enter police station, case number and date of the report under “Police report” (per the report confirmation or the driver’s details in the report). The search in the overview also finds the police case number.","help_tachofault_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe the fault (e.g. the device error message). If the description mentions the tachograph or driver card, the form shows these duties again.|Device faulty: note the periods not correctly recorded or printed on a separate sheet or on the back of the printout – with name, driver card or driving licence number and signature (Art. 37).|Repair only by an approved workshop as soon as circumstances permit; if returning to the company premises is not possible within one week, have it repaired en route (Art. 37).|Driver card damaged or lost: make a printout at the start and end of the journey, enter name and card or driving licence number and sign; apply for a replacement within 7 calendar days; drive without a card for at most 15 calendar days, longer only if needed to return to the premises (Art. 29, 35).|Your driver card and driving licence numbers – if recorded – are under More → My driver file.|Carry and keep printouts and notes; call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|The app keeps no deadline for this. Device faulty: fleet management orders the workshop via the case. Driver card damaged, lost or stolen: fleet management records the replacement card in the driver file as a new “Driver card (tachograph)” record.","help_theft_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened – even if the whole vehicle is gone (it stays selectable in the list). If the description mentions theft or a break-in, the form shows these steps again.|Call the police (110 in Germany, 112 abroad) and file a report; note the police station and case number here in the description.|Do not touch or tidy anything until the police have secured evidence; then attach photos of the break-in marks and the places where something is missing (below “Photos”).|Write down what is missing (cargo, fuel, tools, papers such as the registration certificate, driver card, fuel card), where the vehicle was parked and when it was last in order.|Call fleet management or dispatch immediately (insurance, tracking if available, block the fuel card) – this report does not replace that.|Both truck and trailer affected (e.g. fuel cap broken open and curtain slashed): report each vehicle separately – repair, costs and insurance are handled per vehicle.|Driver card stolen: also see the section “Tachograph faulty, driver card damaged or lost”.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management sorts out insurance and replacement via the case.","help_hitrun_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is damaged. If the description mentions a hit-and-run or damage while parked, the form shows these steps instead of the general accident hints.|If anyone is injured, call 112 first. Otherwise call the police (110 in Germany, 112 abroad) and report the hit-and-run – even for minor damage; note the police station and case number here in the description.|If possible, leave the vehicle where it is until the police have been; attach photos of the damage (close-up and with surroundings), of paint traces or parts on the ground and of the location (below “Photos”).|Write down where the vehicle was parked, since when, and when the damage was noticed; if there are witnesses, a note on the vehicle or cameras (rest area, yard, dashcam), mention that here and tell the police.|Call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management records the police report and insurance claim on the case.","help_vandal_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is damaged. If the description mentions vandalism, graffiti, slashed tyres or deliberate damage, the form shows these steps.|Before driving off, check all around: tyres and valves, air and electrical lines to the trailer, mirrors, windows and lights. If any of these is damaged, do not drive and below under “Can the vehicle be driven?” choose “No, not drivable”.|Call the police (110 in Germany, 112 abroad) and report the criminal damage; note the police station and case number here in the description.|Do not clean or repair anything before photos are taken: an overview and a close-up of each spot, licence plate in the picture (below “Photos”). If something is also missing (cargo, fuel, tools), write down what is missing.|Write down where the vehicle was parked, since when, and when the damage was noticed; note witnesses or cameras (yard, rest area) here and tell the police. Call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|Both truck and trailer affected: report each vehicle separately – repair, costs and insurance are handled per vehicle.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management records the police report and insurance claim on the case.","help_marten_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what has been chewed. If the description mentions a marten, a rodent or chewed/bitten-through cables or hoses, the form shows these steps.|Leave the engine off and check as far as possible without tools (service flap, under the vehicle): chewed cables, coolant or air hoses, insulation mats. If coolant is dripping, a warning light is on or a cable is bitten through, do not drive and below under “Can the vehicle be driven?” choose “No, not drivable”.|Attach photos of the bite marks and traces (paw prints, insulation scraps on the ground) (below “Photos”). Do not patch chewed lines with tape yourself – the workshop will check them.|Write down where the vehicle was parked and whether marten damage has happened there before – this helps management sort out protection at that parking spot.|If the vehicle cannot be driven: call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management sorts out repair and insurance via the case.","help_wild_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe the collision. If the description mentions a deer, boar, moose or similar, the form shows these steps instead of the general accident hints.|Secure the scene (hazard lights, safety vest, warning triangle); if anyone is injured, call 112 first.|Call the police (110 in Germany, 112 abroad) – even without injuries; they inform the responsible hunter. Ask for a wildlife accident certificate for the insurer and note the police station here.|Do not touch or take the injured or dead animal (risk of injury; taking it is a criminal offence).|Attach photos of the animal, the scene and all damage (below “Photos”) and call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management sorts out insurance and repair via the case.","help_glass_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe the damage to the glass. If the description mentions a stone chip, crack or windscreen, the form shows these steps again.|Attach one photo of the whole windscreen/window and a close-up with a size reference (e.g. a coin) (below “Photos”).|State in the description where the damage is (in the driver’s field of view or at the edge), how large it is and whether it is a stone chip or already a crack.|A crack or damage in the field of view impairs visibility: clarify with fleet management or dispatch whether to continue driving and choose the matching driveability below.|Report small stone chips right away too – as long as no crack has formed they can often be repaired instead of replacing the glass.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management decides on repair or replacement and checks the insurance cover according to the contract.","help_lost_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is missing. If the description mentions lost keys, the registration certificate or vehicle documents, the form shows these steps.|Do not leave the vehicle unlocked: lock it if possible or stay with it. Describe here where and when you last had the keys or documents, and enter the location below.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they will arrange a spare key and decide how to continue. Only call a locksmith or towing service after agreeing with them.|The registration certificate (Zulassungsbescheinigung Teil I) must be carried while driving: continue without it only after agreeing with management. The fleet manager applies for the replacement.|Suspected theft (e.g. bag or keys stolen): call the police (110 in Germany, 112 abroad) and note the case number here. Found them again? Tell the fleet manager or dispatch straight away.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager arranges spare keys and replacement documents via the case.","help_flat_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe the breakdown. If the description mentions a flat or burst tyre, the form shows these steps.|Stop safely – if possible on the hard shoulder, in a car park or an emergency bay. Do not drive on with a flat tyre.|Hazard lights on, put on the high-visibility vest, set up the warning triangle and get behind the crash barrier.|Do not change the tyre yourself on the hard shoulder: call the fleet manager or dispatch – they will arrange the tyre or breakdown service.|Note here the location (motorway, direction, next exit or kilometre marker) and the affected tyre (tractor unit or trailer, axle, left/right); photograph the tyre and attach it below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable”.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager arranges the breakdown service, the replacement tyre and costs via the case.","help_roadchk_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what was objected to at the check. If the description mentions an objection at a roadside check (e.g. “police criticised the load securing”, “not allowed to continue”), the form shows these steps.|Follow the instructions of the inspection: if you were not allowed to continue, only drive on once the inspectors allow it (e.g. after re-securing the load).|Call the fleet manager or dispatch straight away – e.g. if securing equipment, a replacement vehicle or a workshop is needed.|Photograph the defect report, warning or inspection record and attach it below; note here what was objected to (e.g. load securing, brakes, lights) and who carried out the check (police, BALM – formerly BAG).|If a hearing form or fine notice arrives later, hand it to the fleet manager straight away.|As long as you may not continue: under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable”.|The app sets no deadline or amount for this – the fleet manager handles defects via the case and records a later fine under “Fines & notices”.","help_nostart_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happens when starting. If the description mentions a starting problem (e.g. “won’t start”, “flat battery”), the form shows these steps.|Do not keep cranking for long – it spares the starter and battery; try again after a short pause.|Jump-start only if you have been trained for it, and only from a vehicle with the same system voltage (a truck not from a car). Otherwise call the fleet manager or dispatch – they will arrange the breakdown service or workshop.|Note here what happens (e.g. starter does not turn; starter turns but the engine does not start; warning on the display; key will not turn in the ignition); photograph display warnings and attach them below. Do not force the key in the ignition or steering lock.|If you carry chilled goods or have a fixed delivery time, say so straight away on the phone.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable”.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager arranges the breakdown service, workshop and costs via the case.","help_warnlight_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which warning light or message appears. If the description mentions a warning light or warning message (also e.g. “low oil pressure”), the form shows these steps.|Red warning light or stop message: as soon as it is safe, stop, switch off the engine and call the fleet manager or dispatch – do not drive on.|Yellow or amber warning light: do not simply drive on – check with the fleet manager or dispatch whether and how far the trip may continue.|Photograph the display or instrument cluster (symbol, message text or fault code) and attach it below; note here when the light came on and whether anything changed (power, noises, smell).|If there is smoke or a burning smell: stop, leave the vehicle, keep your distance and call the emergency number.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” for a red warning light, otherwise “Limited” or “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager decides on continuing, the breakdown service and the workshop via the case.","help_leak_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is leaking and where. If the description mentions an oil trail or leaking fluid (e.g. “oil leak”, “diesel dripping”), the form shows these steps.|Do not set off – or, as soon as it is safe, stop, switch off the engine and call the fleet manager or dispatch; only drive on after agreeing it with them.|If you smell diesel or it is dripping onto hot parts: no smoking, no open flame, keep your distance; if there is smoke or a burning smell, leave the vehicle and call the emergency number.|Do not let the fluid run into drains, soil or water – spread absorbent if available. Oil trail or large puddle on a public road: inform the fleet manager or dispatch immediately and call the police if it endangers others.|Photograph the puddle or the leaking spot and attach it below; note here where it drips (front, middle, rear, tractor unit or trailer), the colour and smell of the fluid and whether a warning light is on.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” for heavy dripping or fuel, otherwise “Limited” or “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager decides on continuing, clean-up, the workshop and costs via the case.","help_custdmg_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what was damaged at the customer. If the description mentions damage by a forklift, at the loading dock or at the customer (e.g. “forklift ripped the curtain”), the form shows these steps.|While still on site, look at the damage together with the customer’s responsible person (warehouse or shift supervisor) and have it noted and countersigned on the delivery note or consignment note – do not leave without that note.|Note the company, location with gate or dock and the time here; ask for the responsible person’s contact details and pass them on to the fleet manager.|Photograph the damage – an overview with the number plate and a close-up, if possible also the forklift or dock – and attach it below.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch; do not sign an admission of fault or anything that describes the damage differently from how it is.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if lights, brakes, tyres, doors or load securing are affected, otherwise “Limited” or “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager settles the damage, workshop and costs with the customer via the case.","help_fire_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe where the smoke or burning smell appeared. If the description mentions smoke, a burning smell or fire (e.g. “smoke from the engine bay”, “brakes smoking”), the form shows these steps.|Stop safely at once (if possible not in a tunnel or at a filling station), switch off the engine, turn on the hazard lights, put on the high-visibility vest.|Everyone out of the vehicle, keep your distance, do not breathe in the smoke, do not stand on the carriageway; with open flames or heavy smoke call the emergency number immediately.|Only try to extinguish if it is safe and you have been trained (fire extinguisher on board); do not open the bonnet or cab wide. With dangerous goods, keep the transport documents and written instructions ready for the fire brigade.|Only once everyone is safe: call the fleet manager or dispatch; note here where the smoke came from (engine bay, wheel or brake, cab, trailer, refrigeration unit), its colour and smell and any warning lights – photos only from a safe distance.|Choose “Critical” below and “No, not drivable” under “Can the vehicle be driven?” – even if the smoke has stopped; only drive on once the workshop or fleet manager has cleared it.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager arranges recovery, the workshop, insurance and costs via the case.","help_reefer_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle (reefer trailer) and describe what the refrigeration unit shows. If the description mentions a refrigeration unit fault (e.g. “reefer unit failed”, “refrigeration alarm”, “trailer getting warm”, “temperature recorder broken”), the form shows these steps. If the temperature recorder does not work, photograph the actual temperature on the display regularly.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch immediately; keep the load doors closed so the cold stays in the load space.|Photograph the refrigeration unit’s display (actual and set temperature, fault code or alarm) and attach it below; note here when the failure was noticed. Keep a temperature recorder printout if available.|Only simple checks you have been trained for (e.g. the unit’s fuel, the switch, a restart as instructed); do not open anything on the refrigerant circuit.|Note here what goods are loaded (chilled or frozen) and when they must be at the customer – dispatch decides on reloading or a replacement trailer.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Limited” if only the refrigeration unit is affected; with other defects “No, not drivable” or “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline and no temperature limit for this – the fleet manager or dispatch settles goods, customer, workshop and costs via the case.","help_brake_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with the brakes. If the description mentions the brakes or the air system (e.g. “brakes squealing”, “trailer not braking”, “air hissing”), the form shows these steps.|With pressure loss, hissing air, a soft pedal or noticeably weaker braking: as soon as it is safe, stop, apply the parking brake and call the fleet manager or dispatch – do not drive on.|With pulling to one side, judder, squealing, grinding or a failed retarder or engine brake: drive carefully – slow down earlier on downhill stretches and choose a lower gear so the service brakes do not overheat – and check with the fleet manager or dispatch whether and how far the trip may continue.|Do not touch a hot, smoking or strongly smelling brake and do not cool it with water; keep your distance. If there is smoke, the steps for smoke and burning smell apply.|Note here which wheel or axle is affected (tractor unit or trailer), when it happens (pulling away, braking, cornering, downhill) and whether a warning light or the pressure gauge shows anything; photograph the display or gauge and attach it below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” for pressure loss or weak braking, otherwise “Limited” or “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager decides on continuing, the breakdown service and the workshop via the case.","help_load_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle (semi-trailer or trailer) and describe what happened to the load. If the description mentions a shifted or fallen load (e.g. “load shifted”, “pallets fell over”), the form shows these steps.|As soon as it is safe, stop (in a car park, not on the hard shoulder if it can be avoided), switch on the hazard lights, put on the hi-vis vest.|Open doors or curtain carefully while standing to the side – the load can fall out; do not step under or next to a leaning load and do not climb onto it; for dangerous goods follow the instructions in writing.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they will agree with the shipper or customer whether the load is re-secured, reloaded or checked; if load has fallen onto the road, secure the spot and call the emergency number.|Photograph the load, securing equipment (straps, edge protectors, anti-slip mats) and damage to curtain, doors or headboard and attach it below; note here what has shifted and where.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” until the load is secured again, “Limited” if it is secured but the body was damaged, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_mirror_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which mirror is affected. If the description mentions a knocked-off or damaged mirror (e.g. “mirror knocked off”, “wing mirror smashed”), the form shows these steps.|As soon as it is safe, stop, switch on the hazard lights, put on the hi-vis vest; do not drive on without a working main and wide-angle mirror on the affected side.|If something else was damaged (another vehicle, a post, a gate, a loading dock), do not simply drive on: secure the spot and proceed as after an accident; if nobody is there, inform the police.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether a replacement mirror is obtained, it is repaired on site or a workshop is visited.|Photograph the mirror, bracket and surroundings (overview with number plate, close-up) and attach it below; note here which side, which mirror (main, wide-angle, kerb or front mirror) and how it happened.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if the main or wide-angle mirror is missing or cannot be adjusted, “Limited” if only the glass is cracked and the view is sufficient, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_coupling_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what does not work when coupling. If the description mentions a fifth wheel that will not lock or a trailer that came loose (e.g. “fifth wheel won’t lock”, “trailer won’t couple”), the form shows these steps.|Do not set off or drive on as long as the fifth wheel or trailer coupling is not securely closed and locked – do the visual check and pull test only as shown in your briefing.|If the semi-trailer or trailer came loose on the road or has dropped: switch on the hazard lights, put on the hi-vis vest, secure the spot; if anything is on the road or others are at risk, call the emergency number.|Secure the semi-trailer against rolling away with its parking brake, do not step between tractor and trailer and do not crawl underneath; call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide on workshop, breakdown service or replacement vehicle.|Photograph the fifth wheel or trailer coupling, the kingpin and the position of the trailer (overview with both number plates, close-up) and attach it below; note here what exactly does not work and when it was noticed.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” as long as it is not securely coupled or the trailer came loose, “Limited” if only the tractor can drive on its own, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_lights_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which light has failed. If the description mentions a failed or broken light (e.g. “left tail light out”, “trailer indicator not working”), the form shows these steps.|If a dipped headlight, tail light, brake light or indicator fails in the dark or in poor visibility: as soon as it is safe, stop, switch on the hazard lights, put on the hi-vis vest; do not drive on like this.|Look at the plug and cable between tractor and trailer only if it is safe to do so; replace a bulb or fuse only if you have been briefed on it and spares are in the vehicle.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether it is repaired on site, a workshop is visited or a replacement vehicle is sent.|Photograph the affected light (with number plate) and attach it below; note here which light (front/rear, left/right, tractor or trailer) and whether it is completely out or only flickers.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if a dipped headlight, tail light, brake light or indicator has failed completely, “Limited” if only an auxiliary or marker light is missing, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_door_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with the door or tail lift. If the description mentions a door that will not close, is stuck or flies open, or a tail lift that will not go up (e.g. “rear door won’t close”, “tail lift hanging down”), the form shows these steps.|Do not set off or drive on as long as a door is not securely closed and locked or the tail lift is not fully folded up and secured – a door swinging open or a tail lift hanging down endangers others and the load.|If it happens on the road: as soon as it is safe, stop, switch on the hazard lights, put on the hi-vis vest; do not force anything shut, move the tail lift only with its proper controls and do not step under it or into the swing area of a door.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide on workshop, reloading or replacement vehicle; say whether any load fell out or may be damaged.|Photograph the door, lock or tail lift (with number plate) and attach it below; note here which door (rear/side, left/right, cab or body) and what exactly does not work (will not close, lock faulty, tail lift will not go up).|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” as long as a door cannot be closed securely or the tail lift is not folded up and secured, “Limited” if everything is securely closed and only opening or operating does not work, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_stuck_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe where and how the truck is stuck. If the description mentions a stuck truck or spinning wheels (e.g. “truck stuck in the snow”, “can’t get up the hill”), the form shows these steps.|Do not force your way out – spinning wheels damage tyres and drivetrain and the trailer can swing out; switch on the hazard lights, put on the hi-vis vest.|If the truck is on the road or endangers others: secure the spot, call the emergency number if there is danger; fit snow chains only if they are in the vehicle, you have been briefed on it and it can be done away from moving traffic.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether a recovery or towing service comes and inform the customer of any delay.|Photograph the situation (with number plate, ground and surroundings) and attach it below; note the exact location here (road, entrance, customer premises) and whether anything was damaged while trying to get free.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” as long as the truck is stuck or something was damaged while getting free, “Limited” if it is free again but can only move slowly or with snow chains, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_visib_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what fails. If the description mentions a failed heater, blower, wipers or fogged-up or iced windows (e.g. “heater not working, windscreen fogs up”, “wipers stopped”), the form shows these steps.|Do not drive on with restricted view – the windows must be fully clear, not just a peephole; stop at a safe place (car park, emergency bay), switch on the hazard lights, put on the hi-vis vest.|Clear the windows with an ice scraper and a cloth, direct the blower at the windscreen and – if available – top up the washer fluid with antifreeze; do not pour hot water on the glass, it can crack.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether you carry on, go to a workshop or a replacement vehicle comes.|Note here exactly what fails (heater, blower, wipers, washer fluid) and whether the windscreen fogs up inside or ices up outside; attach a photo of the controls or the windscreen below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” as long as the view does not clear or the wipers do not work, “Limited” if the windows only stay clear with difficulty, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_loose_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which part is loose or missing. If the description mentions a part hanging down, dragging or lost (e.g. “mudguard hanging off”, “bumper fell off”, “fender broke off”), the form shows these steps.|If a part is hanging down or dragging: stop at a safe place, switch on the hazard lights, put on the hi-vis vest – do not drive on while something is loose that could fall off or damage a tyre.|If a part has been left on the road and endangers others: do not walk onto the carriageway yourself, call the emergency number and give the location.|Only secure or remove loose parts if this can be done safely away from moving traffic; then call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether you carry on or go to a workshop.|Attach photos of the loose or missing part (with number plate) below; note here which part on which side is affected and – if something was lost – roughly where (road, direction).|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” as long as a part is loose or dragging, “Limited” if it is secured but still needs repair, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_adas_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which message appears. If the description mentions a faulty driver assistance system, a camera or braking for no reason (e.g. “emergency braking system fault”, “blind spot camera not working”), the form shows these steps.|Treat the reported system (e.g. emergency braking, turning or lane keeping assistant, adaptive cruise control, camera) as failed – do not rely on it any more; keep a greater distance and use the mirrors and shoulder checks with extra care when turning.|If the display also shows a red warning, the truck brakes for no reason or the brakes behave unusually: stop at a safe place, switch on the hazard lights and do not drive on.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether you carry on or go to a workshop; do not switch off assistance systems yourself unless you have been briefed on it.|Attach a photo of the message on the display below; note here which system is affected, whether the message is permanent or only appears at times and whether a camera or sensor might be dirty or iced up.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if the truck braked for no reason or the brakes are affected, “Limited” if only an assistant has failed and the fleet manager allows you to carry on, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_steer_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe how the steering behaves. If the description mentions heavy, wobbling or locking steering, play in the steering wheel, failed power steering or a truck pulling to one side (e.g. “steering is very heavy”, “truck pulls to the right”), the form shows these steps.|If the steering suddenly becomes heavy, locks, the steering wheel has noticeable play or the truck pulls strongly to one side: reduce speed carefully, stop at a safe place, switch on the hazard lights, put on the safety vest and do not drive on.|If the display shows a steering warning or the power steering has failed: do not move the truck any more, not even for manoeuvring, and secure the breakdown site with the warning triangle if it is standing on the road.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether you carry on, go to a workshop or get towed; do not work on the steering, track rod or axle yourself.|Attach photos below (warning on the display, tyres on the steered axle, fluid under the vehicle); note here since when it happens, in which situation (braking, cornering, manoeuvring, higher speed) and to which side the truck pulls.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if the steering is heavy, locks or shows a warning, “Limited” only if the fleet manager allows you to carry on, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_engine_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which message appears and how the engine behaves. If the description mentions limp mode, loss of power, an AdBlue warning or an overheating engine or coolant (e.g. “limp mode”, “engine overheating”), the form shows these steps.|If the coolant temperature gauge is in the red, coolant is boiling over or the truck suddenly has much less power: switch on the hazard lights and stop at a safe place – do not keep straining uphill with a full load.|If it overheats, do not open the radiator cap or expansion tank while the engine is hot (risk of scalding); only top up what you have been briefed on – with an AdBlue warning only AdBlue into the tank with the blue cap.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether you drive on in limp mode to a workshop, call a breakdown service or get towed.|Attach a photo of the message on the display and, if any, of fluid under the vehicle below; note here which message appears, since when, whether the truck was loaded and whether it happened uphill, in heat or right after starting.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if the engine overheats, loses coolant or shows a red warning, “Limited” if it only runs in limp mode and the fleet manager allows you to carry on, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_susp_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what you notice about the suspension. If the description mentions faulty air suspension, a burst air bag, a broken leaf spring or a truck or trailer leaning or sagging (e.g. “air bag burst”, “broken spring”, “trailer is leaning to the left”), the form shows these steps.|If the truck or trailer is leaning, sagging, an air bag has burst or there is hissing at an axle: stop at a safe place, switch on the hazard lights and do not drive on – tyres may rub on the mudguard and the load may shift.|Do not reach under the vehicle or between axle and frame; only operate the ride height control (raise/lower) if you have been briefed on it.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide on driving on slowly, a breakdown service, towing or reloading.|Attach photos below (affected side and axle, gap between tyre and mudguard, message on the display); note here whether the truck or the trailer is affected, which axle and side, whether it is loaded and since when.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if the vehicle is leaning, resting on its stops or an air bag has burst, “Limited” only if the fleet manager allows you to carry on, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_gear_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happens when shifting. If the description mentions a gearbox that does not shift, a gear that won’t engage or jumps out, a gearbox fault on the display or a slipping clutch (e.g. “gearbox fault”, “clutch slipping”, “can’t get into reverse”), the form shows these steps.|If no gear can be engaged, a gear jumps out, the clutch slips (engine revs up, truck hardly accelerates) or the display shows a gearbox fault: switch on the hazard lights and stop at the next safe place.|Apply the parking brake before getting out – with a faulty gearbox the truck may otherwise roll away; do not force the gears and only recalibrate or reset the gearbox when instructed.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether you carry on, call a breakdown service or get towed.|Attach a photo of the message on the display below; note here which gears are affected (pulling away, reverse, only high gears), since when, whether the clutch smells burnt and whether the truck was loaded or standing on a slope.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if no gear can be engaged, a gear jumps out or the clutch slips noticeably, “Limited” only if the fleet manager allows you to carry on, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_seat_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with the belt or seat. If the description mentions a seat belt that does not latch, does not retract or is torn, or a seat that cannot be adjusted, wobbles or sinks (e.g. “seat belt won’t latch”, “driver seat broken”), the form shows these steps.|If the seat belt cannot be fastened, does not latch, does not retract or is torn: do not set off without a working belt; if it happens on the road, stop at the next safe place.|If the driver’s seat cannot be locked, wobbles or sinks: do not set off if you cannot safely reach the steering wheel, pedals and mirrors or the seat may move while driving.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide on a workshop or a replacement vehicle; do not repair the belt, buckle or seat frame yourself.|Attach photos below (buckle, tear in the belt, seat adjustment); note here whether the driver’s or passenger’s side is affected, what exactly does not work and since when.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if the driver’s belt does not work or the driver’s seat is not securely locked, “Limited” if only the passenger side is affected and the fleet manager allows you to carry on, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_dash_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is no longer shown or no longer works. If the description mentions a failed instrument cluster or display, a speed or pressure gauge showing nothing or a horn that does not work or sounds by itself (e.g. “instrument cluster blank”, “horn doesn’t work”), the form shows these steps.|If the instrument cluster or display fails (no speed, no warning lights, no pressure gauge) or the horn does not work or sounds by itself: switch on the hazard lights and stop at the next safe place – without these displays warnings about brakes, compressed air or engine cannot be seen.|If the tachograph shows nothing either, also follow the steps for a faulty tachograph.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether you carry on, go to a workshop or get towed; only replace fuses if you have been briefed on it.|Attach a photo of the display or instrument panel below; note here what is missing (speed, revs, pressure, warning lights, horn), whether it fails permanently or only at times and whether it comes back after a restart. If the odometer has stopped counting, note the last mileage you read and the day here.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if the speed or pressure display or the warning lights are missing, “Limited” if only the horn or a single secondary display is affected and the fleet manager allows you to carry on, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_exhaust_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with the exhaust. If the description mentions an exhaust that has become louder, is leaking or rusted through, or exhaust fumes or an exhaust smell in the cab (e.g. “exhaust very loud”, “exhaust fumes in cab”), the form shows these steps.|If you smell exhaust fumes in the cab or get a headache, dizziness or nausea: open the windows, stop at the next safe place, switch off the engine and the parking heater and get into fresh air – do not stay or sleep in the cab with the engine or parking heater running.|If you or anyone else feels unwell, call the emergency number.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether you carry on, go to a workshop or get a replacement vehicle; do not repair the hot exhaust system yourself.|Attach photos below (exhaust pipe, silencer, soot marks); note here whether there is an exhaust smell in the cab, since when the exhaust has been louder and whether it happens while driving or with the parking heater running.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if exhaust fumes get into the cab, “Limited” if only the exhaust is louder and the fleet manager allows you to carry on, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_cabtilt_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with tilting or locking the cab. If the description mentions a cab that will not tilt or lower, stays half tilted or is not locked, or a faulty tilt pump (e.g. “cab won’t tilt”, “cab not locked”), the form shows these steps.|If the vehicle shows “cab not locked” or the cab has not seated securely in its locks after tilting: do not set off; if the warning appears on the road, switch on the hazard lights and stop at the next safe place.|If the cab will not tilt or stays half tilted: do not reach or step under the raised cab and keep the area in front of it clear.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide on a workshop or a replacement vehicle; do not repair the tilt pump, hydraulic lines or locks yourself.|Attach photos below (cab, lock, tilt pump, message on the display); note here whether the cab is down, up or half tilted, whether hydraulic oil is leaking and what was last done on the vehicle.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if the cab is not locked or not fully down, “Limited” if it only will not tilt, is securely locked and the fleet manager allows you to carry on, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_gelfuel_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened in the cold. If the description mentions gelled, waxed or frozen diesel, frozen AdBlue or a frozen fuel filter (e.g. “diesel gelled”, “AdBlue frozen”), the form shows these steps.|If the engine stalls, judders or no longer starts in the cold because the diesel has gelled or the AdBlue has frozen: park the vehicle safely (hazard lights, warning triangle if it is on the road) and do not keep trying to start it – that drains the battery.|No open flame, burner or heat gun on the tank, lines or filter; do not put additives, petrol or paraffin into the tank unless the fleet manager has explicitly allowed it.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide on breakdown service, a heated parking place or a workshop.|Note here where and when you last refuelled (attach the fuel receipt below as a photo), whether an AdBlue warning is shown on the display and how long the vehicle stood in the cold.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if the engine stalls or will not start, “Limited” if only an AdBlue warning is shown and the fleet manager allows you to carry on, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_dryfuel_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened. If the description mentions an empty tank or running out of fuel (e.g. “ran out of diesel”, “out of fuel”, “fuel tank is empty”), the form shows these steps.|While the vehicle is still rolling, get onto the hard shoulder, a car park or an emergency bay if possible. Hazard lights on, put on the high-visibility vest, set up the warning triangle and get behind the crash barrier.|Do not keep trying to start it – that drains the battery, and after running dry the fuel system often has to be bled first. Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they will arrange fuel, the breakdown service or a workshop. Only top up from a canister if the fleet manager has allowed it, and only with diesel (no petrol, no AdBlue in the diesel tank).|Did the fuel gauge still show fuel, or did the tank empty unusually fast? Note that here – siphoned fuel (theft), a fuel smell or a puddle under the vehicle (leak) have their own steps. If you carry chilled goods or have a fixed delivery time, say so straight away on the phone.|Note here the location (motorway, direction, next exit or kilometre marker) and when and where you last refuelled; photograph the fuel gauge and attach it below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” while the engine is not running again, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_safetykit_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which equipment is missing or not in order. If the description mentions a missing warning triangle, hi-vis vest or wheel chocks, an incomplete first aid kit or an empty, used or expired fire extinguisher (e.g. “warning triangle missing”, “fire extinguisher expired”), the form shows these steps.|If the warning triangle, warning lamp, hi-vis vest, first aid kit or wheel chock is missing, or the fire extinguisher is empty, used, damaged or expired: call the fleet manager or dispatch before setting off – they decide whether a replacement is brought or the vehicle stays put until then.|For journeys with dangerous goods, also check the equipment listed in the instructions in writing and do not set off with dangerous goods without the complete equipment.|Do not take anything from another vehicle without reporting it there too – otherwise it will be missing there.|Note here what exactly is missing or damaged and since when; for the fire extinguisher, copy the inspection date from the label and attach a photo below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Limited” if the fleet manager allows the journey until the replacement arrives, “No, not drivable” if they decided the vehicle stays put until then, otherwise “Not sure”.","help_wheel_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with the wheel. If the description mentions loose or missing wheel nuts, a wobbling or lost wheel, a hot or noisy wheel bearing or a cracked rim (e.g. “wheel nuts loose”, “wheel bearing hot”), the form shows these steps.|If a wheel wobbles, wheel nuts are missing or have worked loose, a rim is cracked or a wheel bearing or hub is noticeably hot or noisy: as soon as it is safe, stop, switch on the hazard lights and call the fleet manager or dispatch – do not drive on.|Do not tighten wheel nuts by feel and do not remove the wheel yourself – the fleet manager decides with the workshop or breakdown service whether and how they are tightened.|Do not touch a hot hub, rim or brake and do not cool it with water; keep your distance. If there is smoke, the steps for smoke and burning smell apply.|Note here which wheel is affected (tractor unit or trailer, which axle, left or right), how many wheel nuts are missing or loose and whether tyres or wheels were recently changed on this wheel; photograph the wheel and nuts and attach the photos below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” – “Limited” only if the fleet manager has allowed the journey to continue after a check; “Not sure” if it is unclear whether the wheel is affected.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager decides on continuing, the breakdown service and the workshop via the case.","help_toll_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what the toll unit shows. If the description mentions a faulty, red or dead toll unit or OBU or toll not booked (e.g. “OBU shows red”, “toll box not working”), the form shows these steps.|If the toll unit shows red or an error, stays dark or is missing: stop at a safe place and call dispatch or the fleet manager – they decide how the toll is paid until the repair (e.g. manual booking). Do not continue on toll roads until this is settled.|Do not remove, open or disconnect the unit yourself – the fleet manager arranges the appointment for a check or replacement.|If there is a toll check on the road, calmly explain that the fault has been reported and show the case in the app with the time it was reported.|Note here what the unit shows (colour of the light, error message), since when and on which road or in which country; photograph the display and attach it below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Limited” if the journey continues with a different way of paying the toll as agreed, “No, not drivable” if the fleet manager decided the vehicle stays put until the repair, otherwise “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this and books no toll – the fleet manager decides on toll payment, continuing and the workshop appointment via the case.","help_climate_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what has failed. If the description mentions a faulty or failed parking heater or air conditioning (e.g. “parking heater not working”, “AC not cooling”), the form shows these steps.|If the parking heater or the cab air conditioning fails, call the fleet manager or dispatch, ideally before your break or rest period in the truck begins – they decide whether you can stay in the vehicle or whether accommodation or a different plan is needed.|Do not leave the engine idling instead and do not use gas, heating or cooking appliances in the cab – danger from exhaust fumes and carbon monoxide. If you smell exhaust, the heater is smoking or you feel dizzy: switch the unit off, leave the cab and air it; then the steps for exhaust fumes in the cab or for smoke and burning smell apply.|Do not open the heater or air conditioning, do not replace or bridge fuses and do not restart the unit again and again when it shows a fault – the fleet manager arranges the workshop appointment.|Note here what is not working (parking heater, air conditioning or blower), what the control panel shows (e.g. an error code or flashing display), since when and whether it works with the engine running; photograph the control panel or display and attach the photos below.|If the windows also mist up or freeze over, the steps on visibility apply. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Limited” if no break or rest period in the vehicle is possible without heating or cooling, otherwise “Yes, drivable”; “Not sure” if it is unclear what has failed.|The app sets no deadline and no temperature limit for this – the fleet manager decides on rest periods, accommodation and the workshop appointment via the case.","help_window_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with the window. If the description mentions a faulty window regulator or a window that no longer closes, stays open or slides down (e.g. “window won’t close”, “power window not working”), the form shows these steps.|If a side window no longer closes (window regulator faulty, glass stays down or slides down), call the fleet manager or dispatch – especially before a break, the rest period or parking, because the cab cannot be locked securely like this.|Do not leave the cab open and unattended; do not leave the vehicle keys, vehicle documents or valuables in the open cab. The fleet manager decides where and how the vehicle is parked.|Do not force the glass up and do not remove the door panel. A temporary fix (e.g. plastic sheeting) only after agreeing it with the fleet manager – the view outside and of the mirrors must not be blocked.|Note here which side is affected (driver or passenger side), whether the glass is fully down, half open or only open a gap, whether the regulator makes a noise and since when; photograph the window and door and attach the photos below. If the glass is smashed or cracked, the steps for glass damage apply.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Limited” if the window stays open, “Yes, drivable” if it is closed and only no longer opens; “Not sure” if unclear.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager decides on parking, a temporary fix and the workshop appointment via the case.","help_fuelcap_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with the cap. If the description mentions a fuel or AdBlue cap that is missing, lost, stuck or no longer closes (e.g. “fuel cap missing”, “AdBlue cap won’t close”), the form shows these steps.|If the tank cap (diesel or AdBlue) is missing or no longer closes, call the fleet manager or dispatch before driving on – leaking fuel makes the road slippery, and dirt or water gets into the tank.|Do not close the tank with rags, plastic sheeting or tape, and do not fill it to the brim. The fleet manager decides where a replacement cap comes from and what happens next.|If fuel or AdBlue is leaking, the steps for a leak apply; if the cap has been forced open or fuel has been siphoned off, the steps for theft apply.|Note here which cap is affected (diesel or AdBlue, which side), whether it is missing, stuck or no longer closes, and where and when it was noticed; photograph the filler neck and attach the photo below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Limited” if the tank is open, “Yes, drivable” if it is closed and the cap is only stuck; “Not sure” if unclear.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager decides on a replacement cap and driving on via the case.","help_fuelcard_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened to the fuel card. If the description mentions a fuel card that was lost, stolen, blocked or declined (e.g. “fuel card lost”, “fuel card declined”), the form shows these steps.|If the fuel card has been lost or stolen, call the fleet manager or dispatch immediately so the card can be blocked – do not wait to see whether it turns up.|Never keep the PIN on or with the card and never give it to anyone. Do not write the card number or PIN in this report; if the PIN may have been exposed as well, say so on the phone.|If the card was stolen (e.g. in a break-in to the cab), the steps for theft also apply. The fleet manager decides how to refuel until then.|If the card is declined or not read at the till, do not keep trying with a PIN you are unsure of – call the fleet manager instead. Note here when and where the card was last used, or at which fuel station it was declined and what the terminal showed.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Yes, drivable” if the vehicle itself is fine, “Limited” if fuel is running low; “Not sure” if unclear.|The app does not block cards and sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager handles the block and everything else.","help_underrun_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with the underrun or side guard. If the description mentions a bent, dented or damaged underrun or side guard (e.g. “rear underrun bar bent”, “side guard damaged”), the form shows these steps.|Inform the fleet manager or dispatch – even if it only happened while manoeuvring and nothing else was damaged; they decide on driving on and the workshop.|If another vehicle, a loading ramp, a gate or someone else’s property was hit, the steps for an accident also apply.|If a part is hanging down or dragging, the steps for loose parts apply – do not drive on then.|Do not bend the underrun or side guard back, unscrew it or remove it yourself; the workshop assesses it.|Note here whether it is the rear or a side guard (which side), how badly it is bent and how it happened; attach photos from a distance and close up (with the number plate) below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Limited” if the guard is bent but still firmly attached, “Yes, drivable” only for scratches without deformation; “Not sure” if unclear.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager decides on driving on and the workshop appointment via the case.","help_tyre_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with the tyre. If the description mentions a damaged, worn or air-losing tyre, a bulge or an object in the tyre (e.g. “tyre bulge”, “nail in the tyre”), the form shows these steps. If the tyre is already flat or has burst, it shows the steps for a flat tyre.|Bulge or blister on the sidewall, a deep cut, visible cords or steel, tread coming away or rapid air loss: stop in a safe place and do not drive on – the tyre may burst. The steps for a flat tyre then apply.|If a nail, screw or other object is stuck in the tyre, do not pull it out – otherwise the air may escape all at once.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide whether and how far to drive on to the tyre service or workshop; this also applies to a slow loss of air, a tyre pressure warning or a worn tyre.|Note here which tyre is affected (tractor or trailer, axle, left/right, inner or outer on twin tyres) and what can be seen; photograph the spot close up and the whole side of the tyre and attach the photos below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” for a bulge, deep cut, visible cords or rapid air loss, “Limited” if the fleet manager allows careful onward driving; “Not sure” if unclear.|The app gives no tread depth and sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager arranges the tyre service and onward driving via the case.","help_cablock_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with locking. If the description mentions faulty central locking, a faulty or broken key or a cab that cannot be locked (e.g. “central locking not working”, “key fob broken”), the form shows these steps.|If the cab can no longer be locked (central locking, key fob or door lock faulty), call the fleet manager or dispatch – especially before a break, the rest period or parking.|Do not leave the cab open and unattended; do not leave the vehicle keys, vehicle documents, fuel card or valuables in the open cab. The fleet manager decides where and how the vehicle is parked.|Do not force the lock or the key; if the key has broken off in the lock, do not try to remove the broken piece yourself.|Note here which door is affected, what exactly does not work (key fob, central locking, lock) and since when; photograph the lock or key and attach the photo below. If the door itself no longer closes, the steps for doors apply.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Yes, drivable” if the doors close securely and only locking does not work, “Limited” if the fleet manager sets conditions for parking; “Not sure” if unclear.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager decides on parking, a spare key and the workshop appointment via the case.","help_vibr_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what you feel. If the description mentions vibration, swaying, shaking or wheel imbalance (e.g. “truck vibrates”, “trailer sways”), the form shows these steps.|If the truck or trailer suddenly vibrates, sways or shakes noticeably, or it gets worse: reduce speed carefully, stop at a safe place, switch on the hazard lights and put on the hi-vis vest.|Only look at the vehicle from the outside, without reaching under it or between axle and frame: tyres, wheels and wheel nuts, the load and the fifth wheel. If a wheel wobbles or wheel nuts are loose, the steps for wheels apply; if a tyre is damaged, the steps for tyres.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide on driving on, the workshop or breakdown service; do not work on the wheel, axle, steering or drive shaft yourself.|Note here from what speed or in which situation it happens (accelerating, braking, cornering, loaded or empty), where you feel it (steering wheel, seat, whole vehicle, trailer), since when and whether tyres or wheels were changed recently; photograph anything unusual and attach it below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if it suddenly vibrates or sways strongly or a wheel is affected, “Limited” only if the fleet manager allows driving on, otherwise “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this and does not determine the cause – the fleet manager arranges the workshop and onward driving via the case.","help_deck_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is not working on the body. If the description mentions a loading deck, ramp or hydraulics that will not go up or down, lowers, is stuck or is leaking (e.g. “upper deck won’t lift”, “hydraulics leaking”), the form shows these steps.|If a loading deck or ramp does not move as usual, lowers by itself or hydraulic oil is leaking: stop operating it at once, do not step under a raised deck or ramp and keep everyone out of the area; do not force anything.|Do not continue loading or unloading, leave loaded vehicles secured and do not drive off before the fleet manager has decided; if hydraulic oil is dripping, also follow the steps for a leak.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide on loading and unloading, driving on and the workshop; only carry out operating steps you have been briefed on and do not work on hoses, cylinders or valves yourself.|Note here which deck or ramp is affected (front or rear, upper or lower), what happens (will not go up or down, lowers, is stuck, oil is leaking), whether vehicles are loaded and where the vehicle is; attach photos of the spot and of the oil leak below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if oil is leaking, a deck is lowering or the load is not secured, “Limited” only if the fleet manager allows driving on, otherwise “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this and does not determine the cause – the fleet manager arranges the workshop and onward driving via the case.","help_tarp_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle (trailer) and describe what is damaged on the curtain or body. If the description mentions a torn curtain or a wet load (e.g. “curtain torn on the left”, “goods wet”), the form shows these steps.|As soon as it is safe, stop and check whether the load is still standing safely and secured; only secure flapping curtain parts or straps if it is safe – do not climb onto the roof or the load.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they will agree with the customer whether the load needs protecting, reloading or checking.|Photograph the tear and the load (overview with the trailer’s number plate, close-up) and attach it below; note here where (left, right, roof, rear), since when it was noticed and whether goods are wet or damaged.|At the customer, point out wet or damaged goods and have it noted on the delivery note or consignment note.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if the load has shifted or parts could fly off, “Limited” if load and curtain are secured, otherwise “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager settles load, customer, workshop and costs via the case.","help_fuel_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what was filled wrongly. If the description mentions wrong fuel (e.g. AdBlue in the diesel tank, petrol instead of diesel), the form shows these steps.|Do not start the engine or switch on the ignition. Noticed while driving: stop safely and switch off the engine immediately.|Do not drive on: hazard lights on, call the fleet manager or dispatch – they will arrange draining or towing. Do not drain the tank yourself.|Note here what went into which tank and how much according to the fuel receipt, whether the ignition or engine was already on and whether you drove with it; photograph the receipt and attach it below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable”.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager arranges draining, the workshop and costs via the case.","help_accident_s":"If anyone is injured, call 112 first and secure the scene.|In the app use “Report”: choose the vehicle, describe what happened with place and time, state whether it can be driven.|Attach photos of all damage and the accident scene right when reporting.|Call fleet management or dispatch – the report does not replace the call. Fleet management records the insurance claim.|Phone numbers for breakdowns and emergencies (dispatch, breakdown service, workshop …): home page or More → “Breakdown & emergency”; drivers also see the numbers for their assigned vehicles, and after scanning the vehicle QR sticker the numbers for that vehicle – each including the permanently coupled trailer or tractor unit (e.g. the reefer trailer hotline). Without network the page shows the last loaded state – so open it once while online.|Owner, fleet management and dispatch enter the numbers there under “Manage phone numbers” (for all vehicles, one site or one vehicle; test and live separate). Apart from the emergency number 112 the app does not preset any numbers.|For the breakdown service: “Breakdown & emergency” shows an expandable “Details for the breakdown service” per vehicle – VIN, make/model and tyre size per axle from the vehicle master data (also offline in the last loaded state).|At night and at weekends: each number can have the times it is staffed (weekdays, time from–to, optionally not on public holidays; for weekend on call such as Fri 18:00 – Mon 06:00 up to two further time spans; optionally also the state-wide holidays of one federal state, e.g. All Saints’ Day in NRW and Bavaria). “Breakdown & emergency” then shows “reachable now” or “not staffed now” with “again from …” (German time, also offline) and moves numbers not staffed right now to the bottom – so at night the driver finds the on-call number instead of the office. Calling is always possible; only the company sets the times. Under “Manage phone numbers” the app lists times when no number is staffed for all vehicles, and public holidays in the next 60 days with additional unstaffed times because of “not on public holidays” (e.g. All Saints’ Day, Christmas).|Abroad: reachability times are German time. If the phone shows a different time (other time zone), “Breakdown & emergency” shows both times and, with “again from …”, also the time on this device. The emergency number 112 works throughout the EU. German numbers without the country code (e.g. 0231 …) cannot be dialled from there – on “Breakdown & emergency” such numbers also show “from abroad: +49 …”. In a breakdown, give the exact location: country, road or motorway, direction of travel, kilometre marker or exit.","help_login2fa_s":"More → My account → Two-factor sign-in → Set up: enter your password, scan the QR code with an authenticator app on your phone, enter the 6-digit code → Turn on.|After that the app asks for the current code from the authenticator app in addition to the password. A code is valid for 30 seconds and only once; if the phone clock is wrong, the code will not match. Turning it on or off signs out all other devices – sign in there again and re-enable push.|Print the recovery codes shown or keep them safe: each works once if the phone is missing. New recovery codes can be created at any time under My account (the old ones then become invalid).|Phone lost and no recovery codes left? The owner can reset two-factor sign-in under More → Users; this ends all sign-ins. Never pass on codes or recovery codes.|New device: if someone signs in to your account on a device that is new for your account, you receive the notification “New sign-in on another device” (device and time). If it was not you: under My account → My sign-ins (devices) sign out that device and change your password. The very first sign-in and sign-ins on known devices do not trigger a notification. The known devices are listed below: “Forget” an old or sold phone there – if someone then signs in on it, the alert comes again.","help_viewer_s":"Read-only means: you can see data but not change anything – no reports, checks, approvals, uploads or imports.|Cases: read all damage and workshop cases with status and history; search and CSV export.|Vehicles: vehicle file with inspection dates, odometer readings, documents and timeline; vehicle list as CSV.|More → Inspection dates, Tyres – fleet, Workshop plan, Workshop load, Maintenance KPIs and Costs per vehicle – with export where offered.|More → Fines & notices: read letters, deadlines and status; driver names and editing are for owner and fleet management only.|Names of drivers (who reported, checked or measured tyres) are hidden for read-only access – shown as “Driver (name hidden)”. Names of workshop and fleet management stay visible.|Not visible: driver file, contracts, insurance claims, spare parts store, Management (agent findings), data quality, telematics mapping, user management and audit log.|Something missing or wrong? Please tell fleet management – they can correct it.","help_kpi_s":"More → “Maintenance KPIs”: choose the months “from” and “to” – reports are counted by report date.|With several sites, filter by “Workshop” (the case workshop) and “Vehicle site” (site in the vehicle master data, current state); both can be combined. “CSV per vehicle” and “CSV per urgency” download the filtered values for Excel (per vehicle: all, not just the first 20).|Per urgency: median and P90 for first assessment, workshop acceptance and report to release – from stored timestamps only, nothing estimated. Next to “rejected”, brackets show how many were only duplicates (the same report several times, not a real rejection). Internal orders (inspection date, inspection defect, winter tyres) appear as “of which internal orders” next to the reports and are not counted in first assessment and “report → release”.|“Workshop time per vehicle” shows workshop, parts waiting and lock time plus downtime (workshop takeover until release, together with lock time); several orders on the same vehicle at the same time count once. Tap a vehicle to open its record.|“Technical availability”: share of vehicle hours in the period without downtime (workshop takeover until release, safety lock) across all active vehicles – cases from earlier months count too, as far as they reach into the period. Below it, the vehicles with the lowest availability; the vehicle list and the CSV per vehicle show the value as well. Workshop stays without a case in the app are missing, so the figure is an upper bound. Not calculated with a workshop filter.|Vehicle file → “Mileage per month”: km driven per month from the stored odometer readings (last reading in the month minus the last one before). “since reading of …” means the value covers several months; after the odometer went down (e.g. tachograph replaced) there is no value. Record km regularly (departure check or “Record odometer”) or automatically via telematics (one reading per day), then the monthly values are exact.|“Parts delivery date reliability”: entered delivery date versus “Repair resumed” – only meaningful if the workshop enters the supplier’s date when waiting for parts. This rates the delivery, not a person.|“Cost approvals” (only if an approval limit is set): how often the limit was exceeded, how long approval took and what is still open – no amounts.|“Defect repair after inspections”: inspections with major defects in the period (by inspection date), whether a repair order was created, days until technical release and whether the re-inspection was entered or showed defects again. The “Completed inspections” list (More → Inspection dates) shows the same per inspection.","help_costlimit_s":"The owner can enter a limit per order in € net under Settings (empty = off) – the app does not set an amount.|If the valued costs (actual + estimate) exceed the limit, the order shows “Cost approval pending” and fleet management is notified. Items without a price are not counted.|Fleet management: open the order → “Approve costs” with a note. The approval covers the current amount; if costs rise above it, a new approval is required. While cost approval is pending, the vehicle cannot be technically released.|Cost estimate from an external workshop: first upload the PDF or photo to the case via “Add photos/PDF”, then enter the amount under “External invoice” (tick “Estimate”) and select the file under “Supporting document”. Under “Approve costs” the supporting documents of external services are listed as links above the note.|Workshop: you only see “Cost approval pending”, no amounts. Further major work or orders only after approval – do not postpone safety-relevant work.","help_fines_s":"More → Fines & notices → “Record letter”: type, authority, time of offence (German local time as in the letter – even on a device in another time zone), plate as stated in the letter and – only if the letter states one – the response deadline. The app never sets a deadline itself.|In the letter, “Possible drivers at time of offence” lists documented driver assignments and drivers with a departure check on the day of the offence; confirm the driver with the basis. Without a documented assignment, confirm the driver manually with evidence.|“Inform driver”: the driver then sees the letter under “My authority letters” (without internal evidence) and confirms it with “Acknowledged”.|Move the status on (answered, paid, dropped, closed). Overdue response deadlines are marked red and appear as a fleet agent finding; so does a plate without a matching vehicle.|CSV export and calendar (.ics) with the authorities’ response deadlines.|With several sites, “Vehicle site” filters the list, CSV and calendar by the vehicle’s site in the master data; letters without a recognised vehicle are listed under “no site”.|Typo in the plate or wrong authority: correct it under “Change details per letter” – the vehicle is reassigned (ambiguous/unknown is reported); the plate only while no driver has been identified. The history shows old → new.|Driving ban: if the letter states a driving ban, enter the months as stated (1–3) when recording or under “Change details per letter”. The app shows it in the list and the case and reports it in management while the case is open. Clarify the period (starting when the licence is surrendered) with the driver – they may not drive during that time (§ 21 StVG). When assigning the driver to a vehicle, the app points out letters with a driving ban that are not yet closed, and the driver file shows them too; set the letter to “Closed” once the ban has ended.|After “Inform driver” the letter shows when the driver was informed, whether the push arrived on the driver’s device and whether they acknowledged it. If no push arrived (no device, turned off), inform the driver another way. The response deadline does not wait for the acknowledgement – if the driver is e.g. on holiday, still answer the letter in time or ask for an extension. The driver can object in the app with a reason (“I did not drive”); the objection then appears in red on the letter and as a finding in management – check the identification and confirm the driver or identify again. If the vehicle assignment and the departure check on the day of the offence name different drivers, the app points this out before confirming. If an assigned person did the pre-departure check on a different vehicle on the day of the offence, this is shown with the candidate (possible vehicle swap).","help_myfines_s":"More → My authority letters: hearings, fine notices etc. about your trips appear here once fleet management has informed you.|Open the letter, check the details and tap “Acknowledged”.|If you were not driving at the time (e.g. on holiday), give a short reason under “I did not drive – objection” – fleet management sees the objection immediately. Discuss other questions directly with fleet management – the app sends nothing to the authority.","help_tires_s":"Vehicle file → Tyres → “Record tyre”: type (measurement, tyre change, repair, visual check), position (e.g. VA-L) and, for measurements, the measured tread depth in mm (or just the tyre pressure); for a tyre change the tyre size is required.|Tyre sizes per axle are shown at the top under “Tyre sizes”.|More → “Tyres – fleet”: with several sites, narrow down using the “Site” selection.|Limits: legal minimum tread 1.6 mm (StVZO § 36). The owner can enter an own warning threshold under Settings (empty = legal minimum only).|Spare wheel: below 1.6 mm is not a driving ban (the minimum applies to fitted tyres), but it must not be fitted – the app shows “Spare wheel worn – do not fit” (yellow) and reports it as a separate finding to replace it.|More → Tyres – fleet: vehicles below the minimum or the warning threshold, with an old measurement (older than 90 days) or never measured.|Only measured values count; after a tyre change only the new measurement counts. Below the minimum, the fleet agent raises a critical finding.|“CSV export” in Tyres – fleet: all vehicles (selected site) with status, smallest tread depth, oldest measurement and tyre sizes per axle including whether they were checked – e.g. as a list for the tyre service.|Winter tyres (trucks): when changing tyres or doing a visual check, choose “Winter marking” – Alpine symbol (mountain with snowflake), M+S only or none. The vehicle file shows “Winter: compliant” when the steering and drive axles (with twin tyres also inner ones, if recorded) carry the Alpine symbol; Tyres – fleet filters “Winter not compliant” and “Winter not recorded”. A tyre change without this information counts as not recorded.|Tyre age: enter the DOT code at tyre change (last 4 digits = production week and year, e.g. 2324). The vehicle file shows the age per position; under Settings enter “Tyre age: notice from years” (empty = off), then the app marks older tyres and Tyres – fleet gets the tab “Older than … years”. A change without DOT means age unknown.|Winter tyres on the start pages (information only, no lock): owner/fleet management under “To do today”, workshop as a work queue, dispatch as a notice, drivers under “My vehicle”. Based only on the recorded tyres.|“Record tyre” → “Same details for further positions”: record several positions at once (e.g. changing to winter tyres with size, DOT and winter marking). Each position gets its own entry; if any detail is invalid, nothing is saved.|The position list depends on the vehicle type: trucks show front, lift and rear axle (VA/LA/HA), trailers only the trailer axles (AH1–AH3), both with spare. Always record trailer tyres in the trailer’s record.|Owner/fleet management: in the vehicle file → Tyres, when winter tyres are “not compliant” or “not fully recorded”, create a workshop order directly (workshop, optional date). The order lists the positions; the workshop start page, dispatch, “To do today” and Tyres – fleet then show the order number. It is only resolved once the tyres are recorded including the winter marking.|In the workshop plan and dispatch, winter tyre orders carry the label “❄ Winter tyres” – with a date on the planned day, otherwise under “Without date”.|Workshop: “Send to final inspection” on a winter tyre order requires explicit confirmation while the winter status is not compliant according to the tyre records – the status is recorded in the history.|If a winter tyre order was released within the last 60 days but the winter status is still open according to the tyre records, the vehicle file and Tyres – fleet show “closed, status still open” (“To do today” and the winter lists only name vehicles that are “not compliant”) – then record the tyres or create a new order.|Tyres – fleet also lists these vehicles in the tab “Winter order closed, status open” – only when there are any.|After saving, “Record tyre” stays open: type and odometer stay, the position moves on by one, measured values are empty – so many positions can be recorded one after another. From Tyres – fleet and the winter lists the vehicle file opens directly at the Tyres section. The tab “no current measurement” also covers vehicles whose tyres have not been measured since the last change.|Winter tyre demand: on the “Winter not compliant” (or “Winter not recorded”) tab, “Winter tyre demand by size” lists positions without the Alpine symbol per axle and stored size with plates – e.g. for the request to the tyre service (also in print). Only recorded data counts; unrecorded positions are shown separately below, check “unverified” sizes on the vehicle before ordering.|Winter tyre orders for several trucks (owner, fleet management): on the “Winter not compliant” or “Winter not recorded” tab, open “Winter tyre orders for several trucks” at the bottom, choose trucks, a workshop (required) and optionally a date – each truck gets a released workshop order as in the vehicle file. Trucks with an open order cannot be selected; sending again creates no duplicates.|Tyre service converts several trucks in one day (workshop, owner, fleet management): in the winter tyre order tap “Record conversion ›”, or in the vehicle file under Tyres → Winter tyres “Record conversion”. The form is prepared: type (change or visual check), the open positions of one axle and the link to the order; you choose the winter marking yourself (required), the stored tyre size of the axle can be taken over with a button. After saving, the next axle follows; once the winter status is compliant, a link leads back to the order.","help_costs_s":"More → Costs per vehicle: choose the period “From/To” (at most 36 months) and “Show”. An amount counts in the month it was booked (work day or when the item was recorded).|Quick selection above the report: “Previous month” (monthly check), “Current year”, “Previous year” (January–December, e.g. for the annual accounts) and “Last 12 months”. Labour is valued at the hourly rate valid on the work day if the owner kept the previous rate when changing it (Settings).|“Documented” and “Estimated” are shown separately. Missing prices never count as €0 but as “unvalued” (red); if there are only unvalued or only estimated items, “Documented” shows “–” instead of €0 and no cost per km is calculated; if unvalued or estimated items are included, €/km is marked “incomplete”. Without an hourly rate in the settings, working time is unvalued. External services that replace own work and parts are not counted twice.|With two or more sites, “Total per site” shows the amounts by the site in the vehicle master data (case does not matter); the “Site” field limits the report and CSV to one site. Expand a vehicle to see the months with the km driven in each month (last reading in the month minus the last one in the previous month) and the odometer readings €/km is based on. km driven in the period follows “Mileage per month” in the vehicle file: last reading in the period minus the last reading before it if that lies in the month before “From” – otherwise from the first reading in the period (older readings would count trips before the period). No value if the odometer went down. The CSV contains the same monthly values in its last column.|Only owner and fleet management additionally see contract rates and insurance reimbursements – shown separately, not offset against maintenance costs.|“CSV export” gives one row per vehicle and month for Excel. Dormant vehicles are not counted in any report.|“CSV line items”: every labour entry (per job and month), every part and every external service in the period with date, job, supplier and invoice/document – for reconciling with the receipts, e.g. for the tax adviser. Column “Counted in documented total”: no for estimates, unvalued items and own hours/parts covered by an external service “replaces own labour/parts”. Vehicles taken out of service (e.g. sold during the year) carry the status “out of service” in the list and in both CSV files.|Matching an outside company’s invoice: enter the invoice/receipt number or supplier under “Cases” or in the search (magnifier) – the owner and fleet management find the case where the external service (e.g. cost estimate) was recorded. If nothing has been recorded there yet, search by plate and date.","help_access_s":"More → Users → “Create user”: name, username, role (for workshop the site) and – owner only – environment Test or Production. Fleet management only creates drivers, workshop and viewers in its own environment. “Edit” in the row changes name, role, workshop and (owner only) environment; a change of role, environment or (for workshop accounts) workshop ends all of the person’s sign-ins, the audit log shows old → new.|An invite link with a QR code then appears. The app sends nothing: hand the link over in person or let the person scan the QR code directly with their phone. The link is valid for 7 days and only once; with it the person sets their own password (at least 10 characters, letters and digits; no name, company name, “Passwort123” or keyboard sequence). Nobody else knows the password.|Expired links are marked “Invitation link expired”. “New invite link” creates a new one; the previous one becomes invalid immediately. The same button helps with a forgotten password: with the new link the person sets a new password.|After 8 failed sign-in attempts the account is locked for 15 minutes. Failed attempts while signed in also count (old password when changing the password, password/code in the two-factor settings); a lock caused by such attempts ends all of the person’s sign-ins. A lock caused by failed attempts on the sign-in page does not end existing sign-ins – if misuse is suspected, also use “End all sign-ins”. “Unlock” releases it immediately – first check with the person whether the attempts were theirs.|Phone lost or suspected misuse: “End all sign-ins” signs the person out on all devices, including push. “Deactivate” blocks the account permanently and also ends all sign-ins; cases, data and the audit log are kept; “Activate” allows sign-in again, current vehicle assignments and open invitation links must then be set or created again. Your own account cannot be deactivated.|Two-factor sign-in: the “2FA” chip shows who has turned it on; a note at the top names admin accounts in production without 2FA. If the person’s phone is gone and no backup codes are left: “Reset two-factor” (owner only).|Regular access review: “Sort: Last sign-in (longest ago first)” and “Show” (active, deactivated, locked, invitation pending, role), then deactivate accounts of people who have left. The access overview can be saved as CSV or printed. Optionally the setting “Users – alert for accounts without sign-in for days” names long-unused accounts; nothing is locked or deleted automatically.|Audit log (owner only, More → Audit log): who did what and when – narrow it by area (e.g. “Access & security”), search term and “up to date”. The “Audit log” button in a person’s row or in the vehicle file shows only their entries; “Concerns” names the affected account or vehicle. “CSV export” and “Print / PDF” follow the selection. Audit log entries cannot be changed or deleted in the app.","help_exports_s":"“Print / PDF” opens the device print dialog. To get a file, choose the printer “Save as PDF” there (iPhone: in the print dialog “Share” → “Save to Files”). The suggested file name is the page heading (e.g. “Vehicles – CEVIK Fleet.pdf”).|Exactly the current selection is printed: tab, search term, site and sort order. The header shows print time, person, selection and number of entries; buttons, search field and menu are left out.|Depending on the role you can print, among others, the case list and a single case, vehicle list, vehicle file and timeline, inspection dates, workshop plan, workshop load, fines and a single fine, drivers and the driver file, contracts, stock list (shown items with stock value), reorder proposal, item file with history, stock journal, insurance claims, download deadlines (driver cards/vehicle units), data quality, defect statistics, tyres – fleet, telematics mapping, Management, costs per vehicle, maintenance KPIs, QR labels, the quick guide for drivers (notice in DE/UK/EN, More → Quick guide for drivers) and the paper form of the departure check. The vehicle file always prints “Previous completions” and “Open agent findings”, even when collapsed, and the driver file likewise its history (documents, readouts, licence checks); reports state the period and site in the header. Long histories show the newest entries (timeline up to 500, driver file 100 each, checks in the vehicle file 50, inspections and services 200, tyre history 200, data quality 200 per check); if older ones exist, a note appears above them and in the printout – for older timeline entries choose a section at the top.|“CSV export” downloads a file for Excel: UTF-8, semicolon, decimal comma – a double click opens it in German Excel with umlauts intact. The export follows the same filters and permissions as the screen.|Agent findings (Management), data quality and the access overview (More → Users) are generated as CSV directly in the browser – labels and messages in your app language. The access overview can also be printed there; “Show” narrows the list to active, deactivated or locked accounts, pending invitations or one role (CSV and printout follow the selection); “Sort: Last sign-in” lists accounts without a recorded sign-in and those unused the longest first – suitable for regular access reviews. The “Audit log” button per account (owner only) shows all log entries for this person – what they triggered and what concerns their account (sign-ins, role changes, locks); the log CSV export follows this selection. The same exists per vehicle (vehicle file → “Audit log”); the log can be printed with “Print / PDF”, the selection shown in the print note. Actions and details appear in the app language (e.g. “Plate: …”, changes as “old → new”); the CSV contains the raw data. Optionally set “Users – alert for accounts without sign-in for days” in Settings (empty = off): Users then names active accounts that have not signed in for longer, and “Show” offers “no sign-in for over … days”; nothing is locked or deleted automatically.|Exports of vehicles, inspection dates and contracts can be re-imported unchanged after completing them in Excel (check the preview first).|Same plate on two vehicles (usually a VIN typo or a duplicate entry): saving a vehicle and the import preview show a note with the other vehicle’s VIN. It is saved anyway – please check the VIN and take the wrong vehicle out of service or mark it dormant.|Fines: driver names are only included when “with driver names” is ticked. If the app reports “The file contains only the latest … entries”, narrow the period, filter or site.|“Calendar (.ics)” (inspection dates, workshop plan, contracts, fine deadlines, follow-ups) copies the dates once into Outlook or the phone calendar. Later changes in the app do not reach it – export again if needed. Event texts are in your app language (My account); plates, partners, authorities and evidence as entered.|Keep exports containing personal data only as long as necessary and do not pass them on. Exports with personal or cost data (e.g. cases, drivers, fines, readouts, costs, audit log) are recorded in the audit log.","help_settings_s":"Only the owner sees More → Settings. Empty fields mean “off” or the default shown in grey – the app does not set deadlines, amounts or limits by itself.|“Test accounts may only read shared master data”: vehicles, inspection dates and odometer readings exist only once; when switched on, test accounts cannot change them.|“Sign out after inactivity” (minutes, separately for the workshop and all other roles) – for shared tablets.|Hourly rate, pre-warnings (inspections, tyres, fines, driver documents, licence checks, downloads, contracts), follow-ups (insurance claims, cases without change), “not signed in for a long time” and the cost approval limit: enter a value and press “Save”. Invalid values are rejected with the allowed range; nothing is saved halfway.|Changing the hourly rate (e.g. from 1 January): with a new rate, “Keep previous rate …” appears. With the tick and a “valid up to and including” date, labour up to that work date is still valued at the previous rate – cost report, vehicle file, case and insurance claims of earlier months stay unchanged. Without the tick, the new rate revalues earlier months as before. Previous rates are listed below the field; “Remove” corrects a wrong date.|Response times per environment and urgency in minutes (receive/accept) – empty = not set; the fleet agent reports overruns only when a time is entered. Exception: cases with priority “critical” (when receiving, also those with a safety lock) are reported immediately, without a time limit.|“Import vehicle master data (CSV)” and “Import tyre sizes (CSV)”: choose the file, check the preview (new/changed/unchanged, skipped rows with reason), then “Apply”. For vehicle master data, empty cells keep the existing data; vehicles taken out of service stay out of service.|“Download backup” saves the app data as a JSON file on this device – store it securely, it contains personal data.|More → Setup status (owner only) lists what is still open before or during operation – e.g. “Breakdown & emergency” numbers in production, two-factor sign-in, push on your device, a backup in the last month. Each item shows its state and what to do; tapping it opens the matching page. The page only reads your data and settings, changes nothing and sets no values.|Working time – maximum hours per user account and day: empty = 24 h across all orders. Raise only if several mechanics book under one shared workshop account; the “Log hours” form shows the hours already recorded that day and what remains.","help_storm_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is damaged. If the description mentions a storm, hail, a fallen tree or branch or flooding, the form shows these steps.|If the vehicle stood in water or water got into the engine bay or air intake: do not start the engine – water drawn in can destroy the engine when starting. Below under “Can the vehicle be driven?” choose “No, not drivable”.|If a tree or branch is lying on the vehicle or lines are hanging down: keep your distance, do not clear anything yourself, call the fire brigade (112) if there is danger.|Attach photos of the damage and the surroundings (tree, water level, hailstones) before anything is moved (below “Photos”); note the place, time and type of storm.|If several vehicles are affected, tell fleet management – they can create the same damage for several vehicles at once as a “Group report: same damage”. Call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management sorts out repair and insurance via the case.","help_bridge_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened. If the description mentions hitting a bridge, underpass, height barrier, petrol station canopy or porch roof or the clearance height, the form shows these steps.|Stop immediately, switch on the hazard lights and secure the scene; if anyone is injured, call 112 first. With a bridge or underpass always call the police (110, abroad 112) – even if only the body seems damaged: the bridge may be damaged and endanger rail or road traffic. If there is a sign on the bridge with a bridge number or emergency number, give it to the police.|Do not drive on or reverse while the body, tarpaulin, roof or load is wedged or parts are hanging down; only remove fallen parts from the road if you are not putting yourself at risk. If the body, roof, tarpaulin or load is damaged: below under “Can the vehicle be driven?” choose “No, not drivable”.|Even with damage to a petrol station canopy, porch roof, gate or car park entrance, do not drive away without informing the operator or the police – otherwise it counts as a hit-and-run.|Attach photos of the vehicle, the bridge or roof and the sign showing the height (below “Photos”); note the place, street and the height shown on the sign. Call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management sorts out repair, police report and insurance via the case.","help_tow_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened. If the description mentions towing or moving by the authorities or police, a pound or a wheel clamp, the form shows these steps.|If the vehicle is no longer there: first ask the police (local station or 110) whether it was towed away or moved. If it was not towed, proceed as for a theft.|Do not remove a wheel clamp or other lock yourself and do not drive off with it. Photograph any note, sticker or letter on the vehicle (below “Photos”); note the place, the pound and the reference number.|Collect and pay only as agreed with fleet management; the registration document and ID are usually required. When collecting, walk round the vehicle – show any new damage (including from towing) on the spot, photograph it and have it recorded in writing; with refrigerated goods check the temperature.|Call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|The app keeps no deadline and states no costs for this – fleet management sorts out collection, costs and any fine notice via the case.","help_stow_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened. If the description mentions stowaways or people hiding in the load space, trailer or container, the form shows these steps.|Stay calm and do not put yourself at risk; do not open the load space on your own. Call the police immediately (110, abroad 112) and give the location and vehicle; if there are signs of danger to life (refrigerated or tank body, heat, no response) call 112 at once.|Do not move the vehicle until the police have arrived; do not alter seals, tarpaulin, doors or locks.|Note when and where the vehicle was last checked, locked or parked (e.g. rest area, port, border); only photograph damage to the tarpaulin, seal or doors if it is safe to do so (below “Photos”).|Call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management sorts out the police report, load and insurance via the case.","help_leaver_s":"On the last working day download the driver card and record it in the “Driver file” with “Record download”; vehicle mass memory as usual.|Sort out the driver’s open reports and fine cases (Cases, Fines & notices) beforehand – questions to the driver reach nobody once the account is deactivated.|Keys, fuel card and registration certificate are collected outside the app; update changed storage places in the vehicle notes.|More → Users: edit the account and untick “active”. The app ends assignments and sessions and lists ended assignments, open reports, fine cases and the last recorded driver card download.|In the “Driver file” untick “active” (left) – deadlines of this driver are no longer shown.|In the vehicle file use “Assign driver” for the new driver; then check “Data quality”.|Recorded data is kept; the app deletes nothing and keeps no deadline for this.","help_handover_s":"More → Users: who has which role? The list can be narrowed by role and status and printed.|Management: open decisions and “recently decided” show what your predecessor worked on; take over items they had seen there.|Cases: the filters “Follow-up”, “Question open” and “No progress” show what needs chasing – notes such as “I will call back” often refer to the previous manager.|More → Data quality: missing information per vehicle (e.g. inspection dates, drivers, contracts).|Check vehicle notes and emergency numbers: your predecessor’s notes often contain their own phone number.|Notifications: turn on push on your own phone, choose types and quiet hours; turn on two-factor sign-in.|Only the owner changes deadlines, hourly rate and rules (Settings). If something is missing, the pages say “not set (Settings, owner only)”.|When a fleet manager account is deactivated, the app tells the owner what is still attached to that account (incoming reports, vehicle notes, emergency numbers).","help_roofice_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is going on. If the description mentions ice or snow on the roof of the truck, trailer or tarpaulin, or a sheet of ice, the form shows these steps.|Do not set off with ice or snow on the roof – sheets can come loose while driving and hit vehicles or people behind. If the vehicle is already on the road: stop at the next safe place.|Do not climb onto the roof or tarpaulin without fall protection (risk of falling). Use a clearing system at the site or ask fleet management or dispatch where it can be cleared; until then choose “No, not drivable” below under “Can the vehicle be driven?”.|If ice or snow has already come loose and hit a vehicle, a person or anything else: stop, secure the spot, call the police (110, abroad 112), call 112 if anyone is injured; note the place and time and add photos (below “Photos”).|Call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management sorts out clearing, damage and insurance via the case.","help_cargo_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened. If the description mentions transport or load damage, or a claim or refused delivery by the consignee, the form shows these steps.|Have the consignee note the damage or shortage precisely on the consignment note (CMR) or delivery note – what, how much, which packages – and countersign it. Only sign what is correct yourself; do not admit liability.|Take photos of the goods, packaging, load securing and load space (tarpaulin, doors, seals), if possible before unloading or reloading (below “Photos”); for chilled goods keep the temperature printout of the refrigeration unit.|Do not dispose of, hand over or drive back rejected or damaged goods on your own – only as agreed with dispatch.|If the damage is related to the vehicle (tarpaulin leaking, refrigeration failed, floor, door or lashing point faulty), describe that here. Call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that.|The app keeps no deadline for this – fleet management sorts out any cause on the vehicle, the customer and insurance via the case.","help_numplate_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened to the plate (front/rear, truck or trailer). If the description mentions a lost, stolen, fallen-off or unreadable plate, the form shows these steps.|A missing or unreadable number plate is a defect on the vehicle: call fleet management or dispatch – they decide whether and how to continue. Do not simply drive on without a plate.|Stolen or unscrewed? Call the police (110 in Germany, 112 abroad) and file a report – otherwise another vehicle could be on the road with this plate. Note the police station and case number here in the description.|Lost on the road: note the route or place where it was last still attached – do not search for it on the carriageway. Photograph a damaged or bent plate (below “Photos”) and say whether the holder or the plate light are affected too.|Fleet management arranges the replacement with the registration office (Zulassungsstelle) – do not have a plate made on your own. Found it again? Tell fleet management or dispatch straight away.|The app sets no deadline for this – fleet management arranges the replacement and, if needed, a new plate via the case.","help_pfilter_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what the display reports. If the description mentions the particulate filter (DPF), a required or aborted regeneration or a parked regeneration, the form shows these steps.|Follow the instruction in the display or the operator’s manual and call fleet management or dispatch – they decide whether to continue or go to the workshop. With a red warning or a noticeable loss of power (limp mode), stop at the next safe place.|Only start a parked regeneration if management or the operator’s manual provide for it – and only outdoors: not in a hall, not over dry grass, leaves or other flammable material, because the exhaust gets very hot. Keep away from the exhaust, stay with the vehicle and do not abort the regeneration without good reason.|Photograph the display (symbol, message or fault code) and attach it below; note here since when the light has been on, whether a regeneration ran or was aborted, and whether the vehicle recently did a lot of short trips or stop-and-go driving.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” with a red warning or limp mode, otherwise “Limited” or “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this – fleet management decides on continuing and the workshop via the case.","help_step_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which step or handle is faulty or slippery. If the description mentions a loose, broken or slippery step, a faulty grab handle or a fall when getting out, the form shows these steps.|Do not put weight on loose or broken steps and handles; always climb in and out facing the vehicle with a firm hold on an intact handle – do not jump. Call fleet management or dispatch; they decide on continuing and the workshop.|Slippery or icy steps: remove snow, ice and dirt before climbing up or down. If it stays slippery, tell management.|If someone was hurt: give or get first aid, call the emergency number for serious injuries and inform your own supervisor directly (accident at work). Details of the injury do not belong in this report – the workshop can see it; only describe what is faulty on the vehicle here.|Attach photos of the step or handle below and say which side is affected (driver or passenger side, truck or trailer).|The app sets no deadline for this and records no injuries – fleet management arranges repair and continuing via the case.","help_lashgear_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which securing equipment is missing or damaged. If the description mentions missing, too few or damaged lashing straps, edge protectors, anti-slip mats or load bars, the form shows these steps. If the load has already shifted, it also shows the steps for that.|Do not load or set off without adequate load securing: call fleet management or dispatch – they arrange replacements and check whether securing equipment is available at the site or from the shipper.|Stop using damaged lashing straps and put them aside marked – e.g. with cuts or tears, knots, damage from heat or chemicals, a deformed ratchet or a missing or illegible label.|Note here what is missing or damaged (lashing straps, edge protectors, anti-slip mats, load bars, nets – type and number, as far as known) and attach photos below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Limited” if the load cannot be secured without replacements, otherwise “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this – fleet management arranges replacements via the case.","help_floor_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe the damage to the body. If the description says e.g. “trailer floor broken”, “hole in the trailer floor”, “side board bent” or “stanchion damaged”, the form shows these steps. If a door or tail lift only will not close, the steps for that apply; if stanchions or slats are missing, the steps for load securing equipment.|Do not drive a forklift or pallet truck onto a damaged load floor, do not step on it and do not let anyone work on it – it can break through further. If the floor gives way or a side board, side or front wall or a stanchion is broken, do not continue loading or unloading.|If already loaded: check whether the load is still standing securely and whether parts of the body are loose or could fall off – if so, do not set off. Call fleet management or dispatch; they decide on loading, onward driving and the workshop. Do not weld, nail or cut off parts yourself.|If the damage happened during loading or unloading (e.g. by a forklift at the customer), have the person in charge confirm it while still on site – the steps for damage at the customer apply as well.|Note here what is affected (load floor, side board, side or front wall, stanchion, roof – front, rear, left or right), what it looks like (hole, broken, bent, dented) and how it happened, and attach photos below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if parts of the body are loose or the load cannot be secured – otherwise “Not sure” until the fleet manager has decided.|The app sets no deadline for this and does not determine the cause – the fleet manager arranges the workshop and onward driving via the case.","help_contour_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is missing or damaged. If the description mentions missing, torn-off, loose, damaged or dirty reflective strips (conspicuity marking), reflectors or warning boards, the form shows these steps. Faulty lamps have their own steps.|Missing or damaged reflective strips (conspicuity marking), reflectors or red-and-white warning boards are a defect – especially in the dark the vehicle is harder to see from the side and from behind. Inform fleet management or dispatch; they decide on continuing and the workshop.|Clean dirty strips, reflectors and boards before driving. Only remove loose or hanging parts if this is safe without tools (never in moving traffic), and keep them in the vehicle.|Note here what is missing or damaged and where (left or right side, rear, tractor unit or trailer), and attach photos below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Limited” if parts are missing or torn off, otherwise “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this – fleet management arranges replacements and the workshop via the case.","help_sticker_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which sticker is missing or damaged. If the description mentions a missing, fallen-off, unreadable or damaged emissions, HU or TÜV sticker, the form shows these steps. A lost or unreadable number plate has its own steps.|Inform fleet management or dispatch – they arrange a replacement and decide on continuing and the tour. Without a valid, clearly visible emissions sticker, do not drive into a low-emission zone (Umweltzone) until management has decided.|Do not stick on a sticker yourself, transfer one from another vehicle or number plate, or paint one – replacements only come via management. Keep the remains of a detached sticker in the vehicle.|Note here which sticker is affected (emissions sticker on the windscreen or inspection sticker on the rear number plate), what happened (missing, fallen off, unreadable, damaged) and since when it has been noticed, and attach photos below.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Limited” if the emissions sticker is missing or unreadable, otherwise “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this; the inspection dates in the vehicle record do not change – the inspection report and vehicle documents remain authoritative.","help_cabkit_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is faulty in the cab. If the description mentions e.g. a faulty fridge, a broken bunk or mattress, a broken-off sun visor, a torn curtain, a socket without power, a faulty interior light, a stuck compartment or a faulty radio, the form shows these steps. Seat, air conditioning/heating, doors and windows have their own steps.|Fleet management or dispatch decides when the workshop fixes the defect. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Yes, drivable” if only comfort is affected (e.g. fridge, radio, curtain), otherwise “Not sure” – for example if the sun visor is missing or the bunk, a flap or a compartment no longer locks securely.|If a folded-up bunk, a storage flap or a compartment does not lock securely: take items out or secure them so nothing falls down when braking. Do not use a bunk while driving without a working restraint net.|Do not open electrical devices and connections (fridge, socket, lighting) yourself or bypass fuses; if there is a burning smell, switch the device off and unplug it.|Note here exactly what is affected, where (driver or passenger side, upper or lower bunk) and since when, and attach photos below – if possible without personal belongings in the picture.|The app sets no deadline for this – fleet management plans the repair via the case.","help_cableak_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe where water gets into the cab. If the description mentions e.g. water in the cab, a leaking or stuck roof hatch, rain getting in, a wet footwell or mould in the cab, the form shows these steps. Wet cargo or a leaking tarpaulin have their own steps.|If water drips onto switches, sockets or devices or stands in the footwell: do not use them and inform fleet management or dispatch straight away – they decide on continuing and the workshop.|If the roof hatch does not close: only cover it temporarily from the inside with what is on board, do not climb onto the cab roof.|Take out wet mats, blankets and items and let them dry; do not remove mould yourself, report it here.|Note here where the water gets in (roof hatch, windscreen, door, window, floor) and when (in rain, in the truck wash), and attach photos below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Not sure” if switches, sockets or devices got wet, otherwise “Limited”.|The app sets no deadline for this – fleet management plans the sealing via the case.","help_telem_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe which device is not working. If the description mentions e.g. a faulty sat nav, a telematics or tracking box not working, no GPS signal or a black on-board tablet, the form shows these steps. The tachograph has its own steps.|Inform fleet management or dispatch – if tracking fails, they cannot see where the vehicle is; agree jobs and arrival times by phone until it is repaired.|Do not open the device, pull cables or fuses, or re-plug anything – only restart it as shown in your briefing. Do not operate the sat nav or tablet while driving.|The digital tachograph is a separate device with its own steps – if it also shows an error, describe that as well.|Note here which device is affected (telematics/tracking box, sat nav, on-board tablet), what it shows (error message, black screen, no signal) and since when, and attach a photo of the display below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Yes, drivable” if everything else is fine, otherwise “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline for this – fleet management arranges repair or replacement via the case.","help_oillevel_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what you noticed about the oil level. If the description mentions e.g. a low oil level, topped-up engine oil, too little oil or high oil consumption, the form shows these steps. Warning lights and leaks have their own steps.|If a red oil warning light comes on or the display reports low oil: stop in a safe place, switch off the engine and inform fleet management or dispatch – they decide on continuing and the workshop.|Only top up oil if your briefing allows it: only the grade stated in the operating manual or on the sticker in the vehicle, with the engine off on level ground, not above the maximum mark. Do not pour in any other fluid.|Oil stains under the vehicle or dripping oil have their own steps (leak) – describe that as well.|Note here what the dipstick or display shows, whether oil was topped up (grade and amount as on the container) and since when it has been noticed, and attach photos below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Not sure” if a warning was shown or nothing could be topped up, otherwise “Yes, drivable”.|The app sets no deadline and specifies no oil grade or amount – the operating manual and the workshop are authoritative.","help_grease_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what you noticed about the lubrication. If the description mentions e.g. an empty central lubrication system, a lubrication system fault or a dry fifth wheel plate, the form shows these steps. Leaks and coupling problems have their own steps.|Inform fleet management or dispatch – they decide on continuing, refilling and the workshop. If the display shows a lubrication system fault or the fifth wheel plate is dry, do not simply drive on until management has decided.|Only refill grease or lubricate if your briefing allows it: only the grease stated in the operating manual or on the sticker on the reservoir, do not fill in anything else. Do not work on the pump, lines or distributors; wear gloves when working on the fifth wheel and secure the vehicle against rolling away.|Torn or leaking lubrication lines, grease traces on a wheel or problems coupling have their own steps – describe that as well.|Note here what the display or the reservoir level shows, whether grease was refilled or applied (grease as on the container) and since when it has been noticed, and attach photos below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Not sure” if a fault is shown or the fifth wheel plate is dry, otherwise “Yes, drivable”.|The app sets no deadline and specifies no grease type, amount or lubrication intervals – the operating manual, your briefing and the workshop are authoritative.","help_elec_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what you noticed about cables, plugs or fuses. If the description mentions e.g. a bare or chafed cable, a short circuit, a fuse that keeps blowing or a damaged trailer cable, the form shows these steps. Smoke, lights and marten damage have their own steps.|If there is a smell of burning insulation, smoke or fumes: stop safely at once, switch off the engine and keep your distance – then the steps for smoke or a burning smell apply (emergency call).|Do not touch bare, loose or chafed cables and plugs, do not insulate, patch or disconnect them yourself; do not bridge fuses or replace them with stronger ones. If the connecting cable to the trailer is damaged or torn off, do not drive on like this – the trailer lights and brake control can fail.|Call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide on continuing and the workshop. Failed lights, warning lights and chewed cables (marten) have their own steps – describe that as well.|Note here where the spot is (cab, chassis, trailer, which side), what has failed and since when, and attach photos taken only from a safe distance below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if there is a burning smell or the trailer cable is damaged, otherwise “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline; only the workshop repairs the electrics.","help_batt_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what you noticed about the battery. If the description mentions e.g. a leaking, smelly, hot or swollen battery, a missing or loose battery box cover or a faulty battery isolator switch, the form shows these steps. A flat battery, smoke and loose cables have their own steps.|Battery acid is corrosive and battery gases can ignite: keep your distance, do not smoke, no naked flames and no sparks – no jump-starting, do not disconnect or connect cables, do not clean, top up or replace the battery yourself. Do not touch leaked acid; if it gets on skin or clothing, rinse at once with plenty of water; if it splashes into an eye, rinse for a long time and get medical help.|If the battery smells strongly (like rotten eggs), hisses, is hot or swollen: switch off the engine, keep your distance and call the fleet manager or dispatch at once. If there is smoke or fire, the steps for smoke or a burning smell apply (emergency call).|If the battery box cover is missing or loose, or the battery isolator switch will not switch: call the fleet manager or dispatch – they decide on continuing and the workshop. A flat battery (vehicle won’t start) as well as loose cables and corroded terminals have their own steps – describe that as well.|Note here which battery box is affected (tractor unit or trailer, which side), what you can see or smell and since when, and attach photos taken only from a safe distance below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” if acid is leaking or the battery is hot, swollen or smells strongly, otherwise “Not sure”.|The app sets no deadline; only the workshop checks, charges and replaces batteries.","help_spare_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what is wrong with the spare wheel. If the description mentions e.g. a missing, flat or damaged spare wheel or a stuck carrier, the form shows these steps. Loose parts and flat tyres on the vehicle have their own steps.|Before departure, inform fleet management or dispatch – they decide whether to drive without a spare wheel, have a replacement brought or call the workshop.|Do not take a spare wheel from another vehicle without reporting that as well. Do not force a stuck carrier or winch and do not lie under the vehicle to do so; do not inflate or fit a flat or damaged spare wheel yourself unless your briefing allows it.|If the spare wheel or carrier is hanging loose or a tyre on the vehicle is flat, their own steps apply as well – describe that too.|Note here exactly what is wrong (missing, flat, damaged, carrier stuck) and since when it has been noticed, and attach a photo below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Not sure” until management has decided; if they allow the trip, “Yes, drivable”.|The app sets no deadline and does not specify whether a spare wheel must be carried – management, your briefing and the vehicle equipment are authoritative.","help_nest_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe where the insects or the nest are. If the description says e.g. “wasp nest on the mirror”, “hornets in the toolbox” or “stung by a wasp”, the form shows these steps. Mice or pests in the cab have their own steps.|Keep your distance and do not remove the nest yourself: no water, spray, fire or tools, and do not swat at the insects. Warn other people at the vehicle. Inform fleet management or dispatch – they decide who removes or relocates the nest.|Do not drive off while the nest obstructs a mirror, door, fuel cap or your view, or insects are getting into the cab; keep windows and doors closed. If insects are flying in the cab while driving: stay calm, stop safely, open the windows.|After a sting in the mouth or throat, many stings, difficulty breathing, facial swelling or dizziness, call the emergency number at once; with a known allergy use your own emergency kit. If there are also mice or other pests in the cab, their steps apply as well.|Note here where the nest is (e.g. left mirror, tarp, storage box) and roughly how big it is, and attach photos taken only from a safe distance below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Not sure” until management has decided; if they allow the trip, “Yes, drivable”.|The app sets no deadline – the fleet manager arranges removal or relocation via the case.","help_parkbrake_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened. If the description says e.g. “handbrake won’t hold”, “parking brake not holding” or “truck rolled away”, the form shows these steps instead of the general brake steps.|Do not leave the vehicle unattended while the parking brake does not hold securely: if possible stay on level ground and place wheel chocks at the wheels, if this can be done safely. Never step in front of or behind a rolling vehicle and do not try to stop it.|Call fleet management or dispatch immediately and do not drive on until they have decided on the workshop or breakdown service. If the vehicle rolled away and someone was injured, call the emergency number at once; if it damaged something, the steps for accidents apply as well.|Do not work on the brake, brake chambers or air lines yourself, and only release spring brakes if your briefing allows it. If the brake will not release or loses pressure, the steps for brakes apply.|Note here where and how the vehicle was parked (slope, loaded, with or without trailer), whether the tractor unit or the trailer is affected and whether warning lights were on, and attach photos below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” – “Not sure” if it is unclear whether the brake was the cause.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager decides on the workshop, breakdown service and continuing via the case.","help_pedal_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what happened with the accelerator pedal. If the description says e.g. “accelerator pedal stuck”, “floor mat under the gas pedal” or “truck accelerates by itself”, the form shows these steps. The brake and clutch pedals have their own steps.|If it happens while driving: press the clutch or shift to neutral (N), brake firmly and steadily, switch on the hazard lights and stop in a safe place. Only switch off the engine once stopped – do not switch off the ignition while moving, otherwise the steering may become heavy or lock.|Do not drive on and call fleet management or dispatch immediately – they decide on the workshop or breakdown service. Do not work on the pedal, linkage or electronics yourself. If something was damaged, the steps for accidents apply as well; if someone is injured, call the emergency number at once.|If a floor mat or an object (e.g. a bottle or bag) is under or behind a pedal, only remove it when stopped with the engine off. Never stack floor mats and only use mats that fit and are secured. If the brake pedal gives way or the clutch cannot be pressed, their own steps apply.|Note here exactly what happened (pedal not returning, truck accelerated by itself, floor mat slipped), in which driving situation (e.g. pulling away, cruise control, motorway) and whether warning lights were on, and attach a photo of the footwell below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “No, not drivable” – “Not sure” if only a floor mat had slipped and the pedal now returns freely again.|The app sets no deadline; only the workshop checks pedals and engine control.","help_injury_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and only describe what on the vehicle or equipment is faulty. If the description says e.g. “work accident”, “injured while unloading”, “finger trapped in the side board” or “fell off the trailer”, the form shows these steps. A road accident has its own steps, a fall when getting out the steps for the cab step.|Look after the injured person first: give or get first aid and call the emergency number for serious injuries. Make the danger area safe – engine off, apply the parking brake, do not move the tail lift, crane or forklift any further and do not loosen the load any further.|Inform your own supervisor directly (accident at work) – management handles the accident report and medical care. Anyone who is hurt does not drive on; management arranges how the journey continues.|Details of the injury, health and names do not belong in this report – the workshop can see it. Only describe here what on the vehicle or equipment is faulty or contributed to it (e.g. step, tail lift, ratchet, tarpaulin, side board) and attach photos of it below.|If nothing on the vehicle is damaged, no vehicle report is needed. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” give the condition of the vehicle, not of the person – if in doubt “Not sure” until management has decided.|The app sets no deadline for this and records no injuries, health data or accident reports – management or HR handles that outside the app.","help_fixobj_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe the damage. If the description says e.g. “hit a barrier”, “knocked down a road sign”, “reversed into a fence” or “clipped a lamp post”, the form shows these steps instead of the general accident hints. Animal collisions, hit-and-run, bridge strikes and damage at the customer have their own steps.|Stop at once, switch on the hazard lights and make the spot safe. If the damaged object is a danger (bent post, parts or guard rail on the road, barrier or gate hanging down, leaking fuel pump), tell the police or, at a filling station, the staff straight away. If anyone is injured, call the emergency number first.|Do not drive on without reporting it – not even for minor damage. Tell the owner or operator on site (gatehouse, company, filling station, car park). If nobody can be reached or the object belongs to the road (sign, lamp post, guard rail, traffic light), call the police and wait for their instructions. A note left on the object is not enough; driving off without reporting it can lead to a charge of leaving the scene of an accident.|Attach photos of the damage to the truck, the damaged object and the spot (street, house number, company, gate) below; describe place, time and what happened. Give management the contact on site or the police reference number, do not admit fault and do not sign an admission of liability. Call fleet management or dispatch – this report does not replace that; management opens the insurance claim.|Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below choose “No, not drivable” if lights, mirrors, tyres, underrun protection, body or load securing are damaged – otherwise, if in doubt, “Not sure” until management has decided.|The app sets no deadline for this and records no details about the owner – management deals with that in the insurance claim.","help_pest_s":"In the app, “Report”: choose the vehicle and describe what was found. If the description says e.g. “mice in the cab”, “mouse droppings” or “bed bugs”, the form shows these steps.|Do not deal with it yourself: no poison, traps or insect spray in the cab. Inform fleet management or dispatch – they arrange cleaning or pest control.|Do not sweep up or vacuum droppings or dead animals dry and do not touch them with bare hands; open the windows and air the cab. Keep food only in closed containers.|With bed bugs or fleas, do not take bedding, sleeping bag or clothes home or into another vehicle unpacked; seek medical advice for bites or skin irritation. If cables, hoses or insulation mats have been chewed, the steps for marten damage apply as well.|Note here what was found (animals, droppings, chewed upholstery or cables), where in the cab and since when, and attach photos below. Under “Can the vehicle be driven?” below, choose “Yes, drivable” if only the cab is affected – “Not sure” if cables have been chewed or a warning light is on.|The app sets no deadline for this – the fleet manager arranges cleaning or pest control via the case."};
